\par\par \b \uc2 CAIJING-Life after the peg  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,694 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juli 2005\par 09:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 The following is from the July 25 issue of China's Caijing Magazine. Reuters has not checked the story and does not guarantee its accuracy.  \par \par \uc2 - - - -  \par \par \uc2 By staff reporter Ye Weiqiang  \par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , announced on Thursday afternoon that it would revalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by 2.1 percent to 8.11 per US dollar, ending a decade-long fixed-rate regime that pegged the value of the yuan to the US dollar. According to the announcement, the yuan would be allowed to trade within a narrow 0.3 percent band against a basket of unspecified \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with each day's closing price determining the following day's trading range.  \par \par \uc2 Following the revaluation, the US dollar edged down against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The ten-year US Treasury bond interest rate climbed from 4.16 percent to 4.26 percent. And one hour after the announcement, Malaysia scrapped the ringgit's seven-year peg to the US dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The next day, the People's Bank of China invited a number of economists and experts, including Hu Shuli, editor-in-chief of Caijing, to discuss the revaluation's repercussions. The attendees unanimously agreed that it constituted a historic move. "It marks a new stage for China's market economy," said Yu Yongding, member of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Monetary Policy Committee and director of the Institute of World Economics and Politics of the Chinese Academy of Social Sciences (CASS). Most of the experts interviewed by Caijing echoed Yu, adding that the move's significance lay not in the 2.1 percent revaluation itself but in its symbolic departure from a fixed-rate regime.  \par \par \uc2 Officials in China maintained that the revaluation was a continuation of their previous policies. "It has been our consistent intention to improve the renminbi's management system and build a market-based managed floating \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  system," said Zhang Xiaohui, head of the Monetary Policy Section of the People's Bank. "But the process was interrupted by the 1998 Asian financial crisis and other disruptions in the following years. The current move is a return to the preset direction."  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say the immediate effect of a flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  system will be a reduced trade surplus and reduced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. However, most experts predict that overall the current move will have only a small impact on the Chinese economy and Chinese trade. Gao Shanwen, chief economist of the Everbright Financial Research Institute, estimates that China's gross domestic product will be cut by about 0.2 percentage points and the consumer price index by 0.4 percentage points. Andy Xie, head of Morgan Stanley's Asian/Pacific economics team, said that while the minor appreciation of the yuan would improve Sino-US relations, it would have little impact on international money markets.  \par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, in the long run, the new arrangement will give the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  more independence in carrying out monetary policies. In fact, many observers have called the new arrangement a "managed float" because though it gives some freedom to the yuan exchange rate, it limits the range in which the exchange rate can fluctuate. Moreover, since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not disclosed which \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are included in the basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against which the yuan will trade, officials will retain significant room for maneuvering. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will now be able to take the initiative instead of passively accepting a fixed rate," said Li Yang, director of the Financial Research Institute of the CASS. [20050729 071303 GMT]  \par \par \uc2 What lies ahead  \par \par \uc2 The big questions following Thursday's announcement are whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take further steps and in what direction it will move. And here, timing is of the essence. "The adjustment of the system is in the interest of China and is set to happen in the short term," said Gao. "The only variable is the timing."  \par \par \uc2 Most economists praised the timing of last week's revaluation, coming in the midst of favorable economic conditions and increasing international pressure for a rate adjustment. In China, the economy grew more strongly than expected in the second quarter. And in the U.S., the appreciation of the dollar, an increasing influx of foreign capital, and market expectation for another interest rate hike by the Fed set the scene for China's announcement, which came just in time to temporarily appease increasingly shrill calls from the US Congress for a rate adjustment. Thursday's announcement also took many observers off guard, preventing market speculation.  \par \par \uc2 But analysts say that the current revaluation will not ward off international pressure if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stops short of further moves. The 2.1 percent rise falls far short of the 25 percent appreciation demanded by some in the US Congress and even the 5 percent appreciation generally expected by the market.  \par \par \uc2 What China does next will depend on the effects of Thursday's move. According to Andy Xie, international speculators disappointed by the small adjustment may retreat from the mainland market, but they may also continue to pour in speculative money, betting on a further adjustment to the value of yuan. Xie did not predict which case would be more likely to happen, but held that China's exporters, which remain a huge force in the Chinese economy, would emerge as a strong domestic force opposing further revaluation.  \par \par \uc2 Many economists argue that the value of the yuan will continue to rise against the US dollar. According to Gao, since other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to appreciate in the wake of the yuan revaluation, the effective rate of yuan - its weighted exchange rate determined after its exchange rates with all its trade partners have been taken into account - would be significantly lower than 8.11 per US dollar. Given the depreciation of the US dollar since 2002, the effective rate of renminbi may have fallen by about 8 percent. Moreover, the US dollar may continue to depreciate, in which case the yuan would need to be further strengthened. "The renminbi is expected to be adjusted up by at least 3 percent within a year and 5 to 10 percent in the coming two or three years," said Gao.  \par \par \uc2 Guarding against potential risks  \par \par \uc2 With high expectations for further appreciation of the yuan, authorities will need to keep a close eye on the risks arising from the adjustment process, advised Liang Hong, chief economist of Goldman Sachs (Asia). The possibility of speculation looms large. Many experts warn that the possibility of further small-margin revaluation will be likely to generate a wave of capital inflows that could inflate the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve.  \par \par \uc2 To prevent the influx of "hot" speculative money from disrupting the country's monetary policy, analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should reform China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transaction center by pushing for the establishment of a forward transaction market. Zhong Wei, director of the Financial Research Center at Beijing Normal University, said that since China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions are mostly tangible small-scale deals, its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transaction center functions not as a real \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but rather a de facto site for immediate \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  settlements. Instead of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current spot market transactions, the most effective method of controlling \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks would be to develop a well-monitored forward market, where speculation would be most likely to occur.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, analysts say the increased flexibility of the new system will create a demand for the development of more efficient and secure mechanisms, giving China a chance to form a healthier \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. For example, a flexible exchange rate will allow for the growth of market making brokerages and forward, swap, and futures deals, all of which could not exist in a fixed rate regime.  \par \par \uc2 Unlike most economists, Song Guoqing, a researcher for the China Economic Research Center of Peking University, said timing was not the most important factor in Thursday's revaluation. Any adjustment, no matter when it is made, brings risks, said Song. But he agrees that the success of China's new exchange rate regime will depend on whether authorities can develop effective tools to resolve \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks as they arise.  \par \par \uc2 "The month-on-month industrial production has slumped and if this trend continued, next year's GDP will not reach 8 percent," Song said. Since the yuan revaluation will affect exports, Song claims that stimulating domestic demand will be an urgent task for China to take on as it prevents its economy from moving downward.  \par \par \uc2 In the coming one to two years, the government can further lower interest rates and adopt fiscal policies to stimulate the economy. More importantly, the government must take a series of measures to counteract disruptive capital influxes and imbalances between deposit and investment that might disrupt the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Improving the social security network and putting more government funds into public consumption instead of investment would help to resolve these imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 The current revaluation has many people looking to Japan's 1985 economic crisis. But analysts are quick to reassure that China will not be caught in a downturn like the one Japan suffered after signing the Plaza Accord because the two countries are different in many aspects. Unlike Japan, the Chinese economy remains "young" and has much room for further growth and the effective rate of yuan will be raised gradually. Moreover, China's economic bubbles are surfacing only in a limited number of regions and have already begun to burst. And finally, the control China has maintained over its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will allow authorities to correct themselves should they make any missteps along the way.  \par \par \uc2 "We are confident that we will keep the yuan relatively stable around a reasonable point," said Yi Gang, assistant to governor of People's Bank. English version by Xin Zhiming (US$ 1 = 8.11 yuan) [20050729 071408 GMT]  \par \par \uc2 CAIJING-REVALUATION|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|O|OIL|Z|RNP|DNP|PCM|PEN|CAIJING-REVALUATION =2|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|O|OIL|Z|RNP|DNP|PCM|PEN|chained  \par