\par\par \b \uc2 House Passes China Trade Bill; \uc2 CAFTA\uc2  Vote Seen Late Wed\b0\par\par\uc2 698 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 2005\par 23:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 )--The House Wednesday passed a bill aimed at strengthening enforcement of U.S. trade agreements with China, paving the way for a vote on the controversial \uc2 Central American Free Trade Agreement\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Trade Rights Enforcement Act, introduced by Rep. Phil English, R-Penn., and Ways and Means Chairman Bill Thomas, R-Calif., was approved in a 255 to 168 vote. The legislation was part of a deal to persuade English and some other Republicans to support \uc2 CAFTA\uc2 , expected to come for a House vote later Wednesday. \uc2 CAFTA\uc2  would eventually eliminate tariffs and other trade barriers between the U.S. and Costa Rica, El Salvador, Guatemala, Honduras, Nicaragua and the Dominican Republic.\par \par \uc2 Republican leaders said they also hoped to defuse some pressure for harsher restrictions on trade with China embodied in bills working through the Senate.\par \par \uc2 "This bill sends a strong signal that the U.S. will not sit idly by while China's mercantilist trade policies hurt our employers, particularly in the manufacturing industry," English said during debate on the House floor.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have been united in their opposition to \uc2 CAFTA\uc2 , and Democratic leaders had urged members to vote against both that treaty and the China legislation.\par \par \uc2 "There is nothing in this bill that would hold China accountable to its international commitments on trade," said Ben Cardin of Maryland, the ranking Democrat on the House Ways and Means Committee. Cardin said U.S. companies had told him they weren't satisfied with it, including \uc2 Nucor Corp\uc2 . (NUE), a Charlotte, N.C.-based steel company.\par \par \uc2 The bill would broaden U.S. penalties to offset subsidized imports from non-market economies like China. It mandates several new executive branch reports on China's compliance on previous commitments to liberalize trade, requires the \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2  to define \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation and provides new funds for the U.S. Trade Representative's Asia divisions and the \uc2 International Trade Commission\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For a decade, the U.S. trade deficit with China has steadily risen. At $72.5 billion in the first five months of this year, it was up by a third over the same period last year, and analysts predict it will continue to grow. An international agreement to dismantle global textile quotas at the end of last year should leave China the dominant global producer of clothing and textiles in just a few years.\par \par \uc2 The large U.S. trade deficit, coupled with fitful growth in payrolls, has fed negative sentiment toward trade liberalization in Congress, especially trade with China.\par \par \uc2 Discontent with trade has created a tough environment for the Bush administration and Republican congressional leaders to push through \uc2 CAFTA\uc2 , aside from its opponents in the sugar and textile industries.\par \par \uc2 English and other Republicans representing steel-producing districts had withheld their support for \uc2 CAFTA\uc2  until after they were assured of a chance to vote on a bill to address China trade concerns.\par \par \uc2 In the Senate, pending legislation sponsored by Sen. Charles Schumer, D-N.Y., and \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , R-S.C., would impose punitive tariffs on Chinese imports to offset what they allege is a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg that undervalues the yuan. That bill drew more than two-thirds support when it was attached as an amendment to another unrelated bill. Schumer and Graham said they agreed to withdraw it until the end of this year, after they were convinced by U.S. Treasury Secretary \uc2 John Snow\uc2  the Chinese intended to reform their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system.\par \par \uc2 Chairman Thomas has called the Schumer-Lindsey legislation the "judge, jury, and hangman, all in one."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  narrowly manages its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a system of capital controls. Last week, the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allowed the yuan to strengthen 2% in a shift from a dollar peg to one against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Chinese government said it would allow a gradual appreciation of the yuan in the future.\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials from across the government and from both parties welcomed the announcement, but said it was only a small first step. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9295; Elizabeth.Price@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 27-07-05 2109GMT ] \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050727e17r00184\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
