\par\par \b \uc2 Daily Chinese yuan update  \b0\par\par\uc2 366 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 2005\par 12:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, July 27 (Reuters) - Following is a summary of market activity and news articles relating to the Chinese yuan on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS  \par \par \uc2 *** The yuan <CNY=CFXS> ended weaker at 8.1128 per U.S. dollar, down from Tuesday's close of 8.1099.  \par \par \uc2 *** The one-year non-deliverable dollar forward discount versus the yuan <CNYNDF=> was at 3,625 points, implying a rate of 7.7474 yuan per dollar in 12 months' time.  \par \par \uc2 NDFs are a transaction where a forward price is agreed between a customer and a bank, but settlement on the value date is undertaken entirely in U.S. dollars.  \par \par \uc2 *** One-year implied yuan volatility <CNYVOL> was traded at 4.6/5.6 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Implied volatility is a measure of how much the options market expects the price of the underlying asset to move during the life of the option.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan rose to 7.1954 against 100 Japanese yen from 7.2496 on Tuesday, and ended stronger against the euro at 9.7431 from 9.7709.  \par \par \uc2 OPEN MARKET ACTIVITY  \par \par \uc2 - China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will issue up to 15 billion yuan ($1.9 billion) in three-month bills in open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, unchanged from a week ago.  \par \par \uc2 NEWS  \par \par \uc2 ** China's yuan hits post-reval low after cbank acts  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI - \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday to push the yuan to a post-revaluation low against the dollar, as Beijing strives to cool speculation that last week's 2.1 percent appreciation was the first of many, two dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Full story: [nSHA353097]  \par \par \uc2 - - - -  \par \par \uc2 ** China yuan move could cut imbalances--Fed official  \par \par \uc2 HONOLULU - China's decision to revalue its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a positive step that should help reduce global economic imbalances, Federal Reserve Governor Edward Gramlich said on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Full story:[nN26180090]  \par \par \uc2 - - - -  \par \par \uc2 ** Yuan NDF premiums ease, price in 4.7% rise in a yr  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE - The Chinese yuan extended its losses against the dollar in offshore forward markets on Wednesday, undermined by the spot yuan's stability and a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warning against expectations of rapid gains in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Full story: [nSP273549 ]  \par \par \uc2 - - - -  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|Z|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050727e17r0015j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
