\par\par \b \uc2 DJ China Trade Enforcement Bill Stalls In House\b0\par\par\uc2 831 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juli 2005\par 22:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service (OSTDJ)\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Jul 26, 2005 (ODC via Comtex) -- \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Elizabeth Price\par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. House of Representatives\uc2  on Tuesday failed to approve a package of trade measures aimed at tightening enforcement of U.S. trade agreements with China, a vote widely viewed as a test of sentiment toward a free-trade agreement between the U.S., the Dominican Republic, and five Central American counties.\par \par \uc2 The legislation stalled in a 240-to-186 vote under rules which prohibited amendments but required a two-thirds majority for passage. House Republican leaders had said they hoped it would swing some votes to favor the U.S.-Dominican Republic Central America Free Trade Agreement, known as the \uc2 Central American Free Trade Agreement\uc2 , or \uc2 CAFTA\uc2 , which is likely to come to the floor this week. Republican leaders said they had also hoped to defuse some pressure for harsher restrictions on trade with China contained in bills working through the Senate.\par \par \uc2 The bill's sponsors, Rep. Phil English, R-Pa., and Ways and Means Chairman Bill Thomas, R-Calif., could reintroduce it under regular procedures.\par \par \uc2 The bill would have broadened U.S. penalties to offset subsidized imports from non-market economies like China. It mandated several new executive branch reports on China's compliance with previous commitments to liberalize trade, required the \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2  to define \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation and provided new funds for the U.S. Trade Representative's Asia divisions and the \uc2 International Trade Commission\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Democrats influential on trade policy opposed the bill, including \uc2 Charles Rangel\uc2 , D-N.Y., the ranking member on the Ways and Means Committee, Trade Subcommittee Chairman Ben Cardin, D-Md., and Sander Levin, D-Mich. These members are also opposed to \uc2 CAFTA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "This bill before us in a word is a smokescreen and it has so little smoke, let alone fire, you can see right through it," Levin said during debate on the measure earlier Tuesday. He said the bill simply called for more reports and created loopholes making it more difficult for companies to bring anti-subsidy cases.\par \par \uc2 "This bill is about one thing and one thing only, providing cover for members to vote for \uc2 CAFTA\uc2 ," added Rep. Richard Neal, D-Mass.\par \par \uc2 For a decade, the U.S. trade deficit with China has steadily risen. At $72.5 billion in the first five months this year, it was up by a third over the same period last year and analysts predict it will continue to grow. An international agreement to dismantle global textile quotas at the end of last year should leave China the dominant global producer of clothing and textiles in just a few years.\par \par \uc2 The large U.S. trade deficit, coupled with historically sluggish job growth after the last recession, has fueled negative sentiment toward trade liberalization in Congress, especially trade with China.\par \par \uc2 Discontent with trade has created a tough environment for the Bush administration and Republican congressional leaders as they push for \uc2 CAFTA\uc2 , although that agreement represents only a tiny volume of trade relative to the size of the U.S. economy. Democrats argue \uc2 CAFTA\uc2 's labor and environmental standards are too weak, while the sugar and textile industries worry about \uc2 CAFTA\uc2 's impact on them.\par \par \uc2 The Senate approved \uc2 CAFTA\uc2  last month, and the House is expected to vote on it as early as Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 English and other Republicans representing steel-producing districts had withheld their support for \uc2 CAFTA\uc2  until after they were assured of a chance to vote on a bill to address China trade concerns.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday supporters and foes of \uc2 CAFTA\uc2  engaged in last-minute lobbying efforts in an effort to influence wavering members on the eve of that vote.\par \par \uc2 As for China, legislation in the Senate sponsored by \uc2 Sen. Charles Schumer\uc2 , D-N.Y., and \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , R-S.C., would impose punitive tariffs on Chinese imports to offset what they allege is an undervalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg. That bill drew more than two-thirds support when it was attached as an amendment to another unrelated bill. Schumer and Graham said they agreed to withdraw it until the end of this year, after they were convinced by U.S. Treasury Secretary \uc2 John Snow\uc2  the Chinese intended to act to reform their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system soon.\par \par \uc2 Chairman Thomas has called the Schumer-Lindsey legislation the "judge, jury, and hangman, all in one."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  narrowly manages its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a system of capital controls. Last week, the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allowed the yuan to strengthen 2% in a shift from a dollar peg to one against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Chinese government said it would allow a gradual appreciation of the yuan in the future.\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials from across the government and from both parties welcomed the announcement, but said it was only a small first step.\par \par \uc2 -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9295; Elizabeth.Price@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 07-26-05 1508ET \par \par