\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-China's yuan eases as cenbank dashes speculation  \b0\par\par\uc2 607 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juli 2005\par 03:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds market comments, official yuan close)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, July 26 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed fractionally weaker at 8.1099 per U.S. dollar after keeping within a wafer-thin eight-tick range on Tuesday, which dealers took as a sign that Beijing would keep a tight grip on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , revalued last week.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers expected the yuan to move narrowly in coming weeks, especially after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it had no intention of revaluing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  further after the 2.1 percent appreciation of last Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's statement poured cold water on speculation of another revaluation, though it had little impact on trading today," said a Shanghai-based dealer with a foreign bank.  \par \par \uc2 "The market now thinks the yuan won't be further revalued, at least not until after the visit to the United States by (Chinese President) Hu Jintao" in September.  \par \par \uc2 After keeping the yuan, also known as renminbi, virtually fixed near 8.28 per dollar since 1996, Beijing adjusted its value to 8.11 after trading closed on Thursday and said it was tying it to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its main trading partners.  \par \par \uc2 But to play down speculation on further yuan revaluation, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  posted a statement on Tuesday, saying the revaluatoin did not mean there would be more adjustments.  \par \par \uc2 "It does not mean that the renminbi was adjusted by 2 percent as an initial step, with further adjustments to come later," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in the statement on its Web site.  \par \par \uc2 In Tuesday's trading, the yuan fell to 7.2496 against 100 Japanese yen from 7.2421, but ended stronger against the euro at 9.7709 from 9.8822, according to the Web site ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.pbc.gov.cn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.pbc.gov.cn }}}\uc2 ).  \par \par \uc2 NARROW TRADE  \par \par \uc2 The yuan opened unchanged at 8.1097 per dollar on Tuesday and traded in a box of just eight points, hitting an intraday high of 8.1094 and a low of 8.1101.  \par \par \uc2 It had hit a post-revaluation of 8.1089 on Monday and a low of 8.1111 on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The movements, though tiny by international standards, represented a much wider band than the yuan's typical two or three pips before Thursday's revaluation, which rocked world markets.  \par \par \uc2 But dealers said they believed the past three days of trade indicated Beijing had little intention of allowing the yuan to move significantly away from 8.11 to the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 Several said the might keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around 8.1100 for weeks, or even months.  \par \par \uc2 "The latest signs indicate the yuan may be confined to a narrow range of some 10 points from 8.11 in either direction for at least several weeks," said a dealer at a major Chinese state-owned bank.  \par \par \uc2 Tuesday's closing level indicated that Wednesday's trading range would fall between 8.0856 and 8.1342 under rules that allow much more flexibility than the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has in fact permitted.  \par \par \uc2 Beijing has given itself the flexibility of allowing the yuan to move 0.3 percent up or down versus the dollar from the previous day's closing rate since 1994 but has not used it since 1996, instead holding the yuan almost motionless.  \par \par \uc2 Banks designated to trade on the China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System typically do not quote different rates than those signalled by the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  because such action would almost certainly trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 But they said there were no obvious signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 CHINA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -YUAN (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050726e17q0004c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
