\par\par \b \uc2 Barron's(7/25)Current Yield: Revaluation Starts To Fix Imbalances  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,281 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juli 2005\par 22:05\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2005, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 Jul 23, 2005 (ODC via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 \f2 (From BARRON'S)\par    By Jennifer Ablan\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 How far and how fast will they let it go? They being the policy makers at China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it being the nation's policy of linking its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the U.S. dollar, how far and how fast being a revaluation of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Those are the questions confronting investors and economists after China stunned global markets Thursday by abandoning the 11-year-old peg of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, at 8.28 to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China said it would letthe yuan float against a basket of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which could boost prices of Chinese goods sold to the U.S. and Europe. Thursday, the value of the yuan strengthened, but by only 2.1%, to 8.11 per U.S. dollar -- and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the yuan will be allowed to move no more than 0.3% higher or lower versus that basket on any given day.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, trade tensions between the U.S. and China have been mounting over the past two years, and have intensified deeply in recent months, because of China's enormous trade surplus with the U.S., which ballooned to $162 billion in 2004 from $103 billion in 2002. That's the largest imbalance ever with a single country.  \par \par \uc2 The Bush administration and U.S. politicians blame the massive gap on China's policy of holding its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 8.28 yuan to the dollar, which they claim is an artificially low rate to gain trade advantages against U.S.-based manufacturing. China has been accumulating dollar-denominated assets, predominately Treasuries and other fixed-income securities, as a result of its massive trade surplus with the U.S. and its former peg, which required it to buy up dollars and print yuan to maintain the fixed exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan told Congress last week that the China revaluation was "a good first step." Nonetheless, Greenspan implicitly signaled the Fed had more work to do, even after nine interest-rate increases in the past year, totaling 2 1/4 percentage points and bringing its key overnight rate to 3 1/4%.  \par \par \uc2 But China's move is the "first baby step in the direction of assisting the Federal Reserve in the amelioration of the current-account deficit imbalance," says Tad Rivelle, the chief investment officer at Metropolitan West, which has $14 billion in fixed-income assets under management.  \par \par \uc2 "One could argue that the fact that China is now marching in this direction might ultimately mean the Fed has less work to do -- at the margin, that is -- as it relates to what it has to do with respect to the fed-funds rate," he adds.  \par \par \uc2 Rivelle says Greenspan had expressed last week and on numerous occasions his concern about the monstrous current-account deficit, the hot housing market and the low personal-savings rate. "The Fed has made it clear that its mission is to address these financial imbalances. That's when they'll stop tightening," says Rivelle.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan revaluation is, indeed, a small step toward correcting the imbalances. But the news pressured Treasury prices, sending their yields higher on the day. Investors sold risk-free government securities on the belief that Chinese authorities no longer will be forced to buy up so many dollars and thus will recycle fewer into Treasury securities.  \par \par \uc2 The yield on the benchmark 10-year note jumped to 4.28% on Thursday, up from 4.16% the previous day, while the 30-year Treasury bond settled 4.50%, up from 4.39% on Wednesday. The yield on the two-year-note, the maturity most sensitive to moves by the Fed, closed Thursday 3.94%, up from Wednesday's closing of 3.87%.  \par \par \uc2 Lehman Brothers' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  team expects the yuan to appreciate about 7% by the end of the year and calculates that it will push rates on the front of the yield curve between 10 and 15 basis points (a basis point is one-hundredth of a percentage point) and by 10 basis points in the 10-year T-note's yield.  \par \par \uc2 HSBC global economist Janet Henry says it's "unclear" whether Thursday's move by China is a one-off revaluation or the beginning of a sustained yuan appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 "If the former, it may still be enough to quell -- at least temporarily -- the rising tide of protectionist pressures in the U.S., as it does imply greater flexibility," says Henry. "If it is perceived to be the latter, it could exacerbate the hot-money inflows into \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  and therefore increase the amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sterilization that the Chinese authorities have to undertake. It could well deliver both." (When too many yuan are sloshing around the money system, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  withdraws that extra cash through what traders call "sterilization" -- issuing notes and bonds to China's banks to soak up some of that new money.)  \par \par \uc2 Perversely, therefore, yuan revaluation could, at the margin, contribute to lower, not higher, U.S. bond yields, says Henry. Seconds Chen Zhao of BCA Research: "It is economically unaffordable for the Chinese to allow the yuan to move up significantly higher because of the sheer pressure of maintaining a high rate of job creation amid still-intense deflationary tendencies within the manufacturing sector."  \par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs economists say the yuan move strengthens the case for dollar depreciation. They expect that over the next year, the dollar will fall about 10% against the yuan, 15% against the yen, and about 7 1/2% against the euro. And the revaluation should also help U.S. equities, particularly sectors leveraged to cyclical global growth, they note. Conversely, it will hurt fixed-income assets.  \par \par \uc2 All told, Ed McKelvey of Goldman observes that the yuan revaluation "increases uncertainty in the financial markets and hence the compensation that buyers of long-duration assets require for the risks they are undertaking."  \par \par \uc2 That's something even Greenspan would welcome. Last week, Greenspan said that over the past year, investors have demanded less compensation for the uncertainty embedded in long assets. "Risk takers have been encouraged by a perceived increase in economic stability to reach out to more-distant time horizons," he said. But he warned: "History cautions that long periods of relative stability often engender unrealistic expectations of its permanence and, at times, may lead to financial excess and economic stress."  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury debt prices rose on Friday, as buyers emerged in the wake of sharp losses sparked by China's scrapping of the fixed yuan. But it wasn't enough to offset another down week for the Treasury market. The yield on the 10-year note settled the week up five basis points to 4.22%, while the 30-year Treasury's yield closed at 4.44% on Friday, up four basis points on the week. The yield on the two-year note, meanwhile, ended the week at 3.91%, up from 3.86% the previous Friday.  \par \par \uc2 At the other end of the credit-quality spectrum, investors were mulling over the $1.25 billion two-part junk bond offering by SunGard Data Systems (ticker: SDS), the target of an $11 billion leverage buyout. The bonds are expected to price this week with unofficial price talk on the eight-year fixed-rate tranche in the 9.25% area.  \par \par \uc2 ---  \par \par \uc2 e-mail: jennifer.ablan@barrons.com  \par \par \uc2 ---  \par \par \uc2 ---  \par \par \uc2 For Barron's subscription information call 1-888-BARRONS ext. 685 or inquire online at  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.barronsmag.com/subscription/subscription.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.barronsmag.com/subscription/subscription.html }}}\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires  \par \par \uc2 07-23-05 0005ET  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020050723e17n00051\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
