\par\par \b \uc2 TAKE A LOOK-China revalues yuan, adopts basket  \b0\par\par\uc2 765 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2005\par 20:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 China has revalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and scrapped the yuan's peg to the U.S. dollar but an official Chinese newspaper sought to quash speculation that this would be just the first step in a long upward march for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The new yuan rate versus the dollar revalues the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by 2.1 percent to a midpoint of 8.11 per U.S. dollar, but China on Friday said it would let the yuan rise or fall by 1.5 percent a day against \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  other than the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Thursday's move by the Beijing authorities also tied the yuan to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  instead of the dollar. These were the first steps in long-anticipated reforms aimed at letting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  float freely.  \par \par \uc2 For Reuters stories on the move plus reaction and analysis, double click on the codes within brackets. MAIN STORIES >China signals bets on bigger yuan rise unrealistic[ID:nPEK328629] > China unveils detailed yuan trading limits [ID:nPEK315705] >POLL-Yuan seen appreciating 4% vs dollar by mid-06[ID:nL22383331] > EU greets China move,sees no quick trade impact[ID:nL22394317] > TEXT-China \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  statement on yuan [ID:nL21684669] LATEST ANALYSES/STORIES > ECB's Fazio- yuan unpegging not a solution [ID:nL22131263] > Dlr adjustment to shift from euro after yuan move[ID:nL22549677] > \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility to make Asia less \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2 [ID:nL22339029] > Japan tolerating yuan fallout,but c