\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Japan tolerating yuan fallout, but could  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoko Nishikawa  \par \uc2 867 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2005\par 12:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, July 22 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surprise move this week to make the yuan more flexible could not have come at a better time for the Japanese authorities, who had been calling for such action but were also worried about fallout on the yen. Despite a 2 percent jump against the dollar after Beijing's yuan announcement on Thursday, the yen is still within Tokyo's comfort zone and remains pressured by widening U.S-Japan interest rate differentials and risks of political turmoil in Japan.  \par \par \uc2 That, however, doesn't mean Japan won't end its 15-month hiatus from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , should the yen's rise gather pace and threaten Japan's fragile economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 "There is no change in our stance that we will take appropriate action if there are any movements that are not in line with economic fundamentals," a senior Japanese Finance Ministry official told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 His comments were the strongest verbal warning about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Tokyo since Beijing decided to ditch the yuan's dollar peg and link the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , allowing it to rise by an initial 2.1 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Beijing's announcement helped push up the yen to around 109.87 to the dollar <JPY=> from near 112, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's biggest one-day gain in more than three years.  \par \par \uc2 However, the Japanese authorities are thought to have refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to halt the yen's rise, unlike their South Korean and Singaporean counterparts.  \par \par \uc2 After all, at around 111 to the dollar on Friday, the yen is far closer to its 14-month lows near 113.75 set earlier this week than to the five-year highs around 101.65 set in January.  \par \par \uc2 Traders say the yen remains under pressure as Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi could call a snap election due to ruling party strife over a bill to reform the postal system.  \par \par \uc2 Prospects of the U.S. Federal Reserve raising its 3.25 percent target for overnight funds by a quarter point in early August, compared with virtually zero rates in Japan, have also tempered appetite for the yen.  \par \par \uc2 For now, the authorities are content to wait and see.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki told a news conference on Friday that it was natural for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fluctuate after a big event like China's yuan reform, but stressed that he remained vigilant over market movements.  \par \par \uc2 "We just saw a big move on (China's) \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, so we need to carefully monitor market movements," Tanigaki said, adding that China had just begun a managed float system.  \par \par \uc2 LINE IN THE SAND  \par \par \uc2 The question in the market is: at what point would the authorities start to lose their temper?  \par \par \uc2 "So far, it seems the yuan reform isn't going to cause an excessive yen rise. So I don't think they will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  now," said Takao Hattori, chief global strategist at UFJ Tsubasa Securities.  \par \par \uc2 "But if the yen's appreciation accelerates after the dollar hits 108 or 107 yen levels, which are probably break-even points for speculators, that move would not be in line with economic fundamentals ... If that happens, authorities will probably stop such a move," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo has made no secret it is concerned that too strong a yen could derail its economy by hurting Japanese exporters' earnings -- the source of capital spending and domestic consumption that have propelled the economy in the last few quarters -- and by adding to deflationary pressure.  \par \par \uc2 But it has stayed away from the market since mid-March last year after a record 35 trillion yen ($316.6 billion) \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2003 and the first three months of 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Some analysts think the pain threshold is not very high.  \par \par \uc2 Toru Umemoto, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital, said Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which decides \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar fell below 109.50 yen.  \par \par \uc2 "Japanese authorities believe that the yen should not rise in tandem with the yuan as it does not reflect fundamentals. They have repeatedly warned that a close watch on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is needed," Umemoto added.  \par \par \uc2 Much depends on whether speculation of a further revaluation by China builds up and whether speculators continue to use the yen as a proxy for their bets on yuan moves.  \par \par \uc2 Ministry officials said they remained unsure how China would manage its new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime and were busy collecting information.  \par \par \uc2 When Japan started boosting its rhetoric on the need for quick and decisive yuan reform, Tanigaki told U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow in London in early June that Tokyo did not believe yuan revaluation should necessarily drive the yen higher.  \par \par \uc2 This, Finance Ministry sources said, was a way of notifying Washington that Tokyo would act against what it saw as undue yen strength. Proof of that, they said, was that Tanigaki repeated everything he told Snow -- except the yen reference -- to Chinese counterpart Jin Renqing when they met in Tianjin in late June. ($1=110.55 yen)  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-JAPAN-\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|J|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|U|NAT|Z|SI  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050722e17m000xy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
