\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen pares gains after jumping on yuan revaluation   \b0\par\par\uc2 By David McMahon   \par \uc2 805 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2005\par 08:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, July 22 (Reuters) - The yen fell sharply on Friday, giving back almost half of its hefty gains scored a day earlier when China ditched the yuan's peg to the dollar, as the initial excitement over Beijing's long-awaited \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shift subsided.   \par \par \uc2 A day after the yen posted its biggest one-day jump against the dollar in more than three years, investors took a step back to see how much higher China would let the yuan rise after switching its decade-old dollar peg to a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket.   \par \par \uc2 The yen, which has often been bought as a proxy bet on an appreciation in the yuan and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , retreated as Japanese importers and investors took advantage of the two-yen tumble to pick up dollars on the cheap. Some were sceptical that the move would be enough to derail the dollar's rally this year that has been driven by the Federal Reserve's steady and ongoing interest rate rises.   \par \par \uc2 "There's a lot of uncertainty about what this all means," said Katsunori Kitakura, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Chuo Mitsui Trust and Banking. "My gut feeling is that the yen's initial reaction to the move was a bit of an overshoot."   \par \par \uc2 Still, some analysts said the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was set to make further gains, partly due to a perception that the long-awaited revaluation -- an unexpectedly small 2.1 percent -- was likely only a first step for China.   \par \par \uc2 "I don't think there's anybody out there who thinks this is the end for China," said Junya Tanase, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JPMorgan Chase. "We could see this yen strength continue for several weeks."   \par \par \uc2 JPMorgan estimates the yuan will climb 7 percent against the dollar by the end of December and 15 percent by the end of 2006. Under the new rules, the yuan will be allowed to move 0.3 percent each day against the dollar compared with the prior day's close.   \par \par \uc2 The official China Daily said in an editorial on Friday that there would be no significant further revaluation of the yuan. In early trading on Friday, the yuan did not appreciate beyond 8.11 per dollar <CNY=><CNY=CFXS>.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar was up around 0.8 percent at 111.10 yen <JPY=> as of 0420 GMT, after it plunged more than two yen on Thursday to as low as 109.87.   \par \par \uc2 The yen and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also got a boost on Thursday after Malaysia said in the wake of the China move that it had changed the ringgit peg to a managed float.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar had struck a 14-month high just above 113.70 yen earlier in the week, helped by the prospect of further rises in U.S. interest rates.   \par \par \uc2 CENTRAL BANKS EYED   \par \par \uc2 After keeping the yuan virtually fixed at 8.28 per dollar since 1996, China said it was adjusting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value to 8.11 and tying it to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the country's main trading partners.   \par \par \uc2 The move was widely expected to prompt other Asian countries, many of which compete with \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  in export markets, to scale back dollar buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at stemming the appreciation of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 But the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore both said on Thursday that they would maintain their current \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies.   \par \par \uc2 Traders will keep a close eye on Japanese authorities, who have not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since March 2004 but warned on Thursday they would take "appropriate action" if there were any rapid moves in the yen.   \par \par \uc2 The euro was up almost 1 percent at 135.25 yen <EURJPY=> after it fell by more than two yen to as low as around 133.60 on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 The single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was little changed at $1.2170 <EUR=> after climbing 0.2 percent on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 Although China has not disclosed the composition of its new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket, analysts say it likely includes the euro, yen, sterling, Swiss franc and the Australian dollar.   \par \par \uc2 State Street said in a research note that China's action could add momentum to moves by Asian central banks to diversify their huge foreign reserves away from the dollar into other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which would be "clearly negative for the dollar".   \par \par \uc2 Sterling was little moved at around $1.7530 <GBP=>, after it jumped about 0.8 percent the previous day.   \par \par \uc2 Investors barely reacted to news that British transport officials closed London Underground lines after a series of explosions on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 The explosions come in the wake of deadly blasts that killed more than 50 people in London earlier in the month.   \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Naomi Tajitsu, Shiho Tanaka)   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -Yen pares gains after jumping|LANGEN|GNN|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050722e17m000d9\