\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: China Won't Lose Appetite For Treasurys: Analysts  \b0\par\par\uc2 561 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2005\par 23:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 By Greg Robb  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones) -- Bond-market participants were wrong to jump to the conclusion that China would cut back purchases of U.S. Treasurys as a result of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform, economists said Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Bond prices fell and yields rose Thursday after China's decision to revalue the yuan raised speculation that the Chinese government would curb its purchases of U.S. Treasury debt, although the move was partially reversed Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign demand has been a chief factor driving long-term rates lower, and the bond market has twitched whenever it has come to believe a major player might not maintain such a presence.  \par \par \uc2 Many analysts assume Chinese buying of bonds will abate with the change in the yuan policy. China is the second-largest holder of Treasurys, behind only Japan, owning some $243 billion as of May, compared with $165 billion a year ago, Treasury Department data show.  \par \par \uc2 But some analysts think the consequences of the yuan move for the bond market are not so dire.  \par \par \uc2 "The bond market's reaction to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform was misplaced," said Desmond Lachman, formerly the chief emerging-markets economic strategist at Salomon Smith Barney and now an analyst at the American Enterprise Institute.  \par \par \uc2 "There have been other reasons for bond-market jitters of late," agreed Avery Shenfeld, senior economist at CIBC World Markets in Toronto, citing a "firming" of U.S. growth signals and \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate trends. "But the Chinese yuan story looks decidedly overblown."  \par \par \uc2 Lachman argued that, instead of cutting back on ownership of U.S. government debt, China "is going to be in fact adding to U.S. Treasurys."  \par \par \uc2 Lachman said savvy international investors will try to put hot money into China to profit from any future rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the dollars to keep the exchange rate within its narrow band.  \par \par \uc2 Many analysts were skeptical that China would allow the yuan to appreciate much further against the dollar after Thursday's 2.1% appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 Shenfield said even a 10% appreciation wouldn't end China's appetite for dollar assets. "Even if we see a further 5% to 10% gain over the next year or so, there will still be a massive Chinese trade surplus with the U.S., to be offset by \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows from China into U.S. dollar assets," Shenfeld said  \par \par \uc2 Economists at Goldman Sachs said they expect the dollar to fall about 10% against the yuan over the next year to about $7.34 from $8.11.  \par \par \uc2 Lachman was critical of China's new \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  system.  \par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China said the yuan would be allowed to fluctuate as much as 0.3% against the dollar each day. But analysts noted that this band was in place, and never used, during the decade in which China pegged its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "It is totally ambiguous. One has no clue what they are going to be doing," Lachman said. "One knows from their past practice that they are very reluctant to move the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . So the expectation would be that it isn't going to add up to a lot."  \par \par \uc2 If Beijing refuses to allow the yuan to appreciate, China would rekindle protectionist pressures in the United States, he said. [ 07-22-05 1713ET ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020050722e17m0000y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
