\par\par \b \uc2 MONUMENT SECURITIES: Very Small Step For China   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,072 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2005\par 13:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Stephen Lewis \par    Of Monument Securities \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--That the Dow Jones index would fall by more than 60 points on the day when the Chinese authorities finally got round to revaluing the yuan is a forecast that few market participants would have made prior to yesterday's policy decision in Beijing. Yet that is what happened.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 The US market, like markets elsewhere, may have been unsettled by the security incidents in London. It is, nevertheless, hard for market participants to conceal a sense of disappointment at what the Chinese have done. While nobody in authority will say it openly, from fear of discouraging further moves towards a market-based exchange rate regime, there is a feeling that the Chinese actions, revaluing the yuan by 2.1% against the US dollar and switching from a dollar peg to a link with a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket, will have minimal effect.   \par \par \uc2 It falls well short of what G7 ministers have been hoping will be the ultimate goal of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reforms. Further, the manner in which the Beijing authorities have kept the yuan within a very narrow trading range during the first session following their action suggests that they do not intend to be much more flexible than they have been in the past.   \par \par \uc2 A 2.1% exchange rate move is within the normal weekly range of fluctuation between the major free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In itself, it is likely to have negligible economic impact. As a substantial importer of raw materials priced in dollars, China will enjoy a small cost benefit from the revaluation.   \par \par \uc2 This will help Chinese companies keep their export prices steady in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  terms. If the Beijing authorities were to become more responsive to market forces and, having abandoned the yuan's 8.28 peg to the US dollar, were willing to countenance frequent yuan revaluations, they might engage in less \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in future.   \par \par \uc2 Chinese official demand for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  securities would tail off as Chinese reserves would grow less rapidly. These reserves reached $711bn by the end of June and had been rising at a $200bn annual rate since the beginning of 2004. There are no data on how the reserves were invested. Separate US Treasury figures show, however, that total Chinese holdings of US government securities rose by $85bn between end-December 2003 and end-May this year, with a further $24.4bn (net) invested in agency securities.   \par \par \uc2 This compares with total net issuance of government and agency securities of a little less than $650bn in the five quarters to 2005Q1, the latest period for which the Federal Reserve has published data. The Chinese contribution to the overall demand for US Treasuries and agencies has, therefore, been significant.   \par \par \uc2 If it were to diminish, there might be less downward pressure on long-term dollar bond yields. Consequently, US investors have reason to be thankful that the Chinese authorities appear to be relatively inflexible in their approach to reform. Even so, in switching to a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket, Beijing will, in future, be free of the constraint of the dollar peg that has required, up to now, that its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  be conducted exclusively in support of the US dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Future \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if it is to stabilise the basket value of the yuan, will have to be balanced between the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  making up the basket. This necessarily implies that a smaller proportion of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be in dollars. How much smaller is hard to say, seeing that the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not providing details of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weightings in the basket.   \par \par \uc2 For Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on any given scale, the support it affords the US dollar and US Treasuries will be less than it would have been under the dollar peg regime. However, the actual impact on dollar markets will depend on the future scale of Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 This \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be less vigorous than in the past since the Chinese authorities have restored yuan revaluation to their armoury of measures to deal with unwanted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pressures. On the other hand, it may be that the newly-established flexibility in the yuan rate will encourage buying of the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and intensify the upward pressures exerted upon it.   \par \par \uc2 There may, accordingly, be more instances where \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems appropriate. The crawling peg regime that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems effectively to have adopted is out of favour in international central banking circles. It lacks the virtues of a hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg in deterring speculation while it is not sufficiently responsive to capital flows to allow a fully market-based determination of the exchange rate.   \par \par \uc2 The final verdict of the IMF was that crawling pegs had, in part, been responsible for the Asian crises of 1997-98. Such an approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  management is nowadays widely viewed as less than optimal for any country that does not have effective controls on capital flows. Clearly, if China is to liberalise its capital markets, its leaders will have to consider what changes to make in their present exchange regime.   \par \par \uc2 It is in this context that China's move could be problematic for the authorities in Hong Kong. The HKMA has been content to stick with its dollar peg. But if the yuan appreciates in stages, the Hong Kong peg could come under pressure. It would certainly not suit Hong Kong to follow the mainland in adopting a crawling peg for its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . There could be some choppy trading days ahead in the region's markets.   \par \par \uc2 -By Stephen Lewis: 44 20 7338 0179: analysismonumentsecurities.com   \par \par \uc2 (Stephen Lewis is chief economist at Monument Securities Ltd., London, independent brokers specializing in institutional business.)   \par \par \uc2 Opinions expressed are those of the author, and not of Dow Jones Newswires.   \par \par \uc2 This column is published for information only, and it neither constitutes, nor is to be construed as, an offer to buy or sell investments. The information and opinions expressed herein are based on sources the author believes to be reliable, but he cannot represent that they are accurate or complete. Any information herein is given in good faith, but is subject to change without notice. No liability is accepted whatsoever by Monument Securities Ltd., employees and associated companies for any direct or consequential loss arising from this article. Monument Securities Ltd. is regulated by the SFA and is a member of the London Stock Exchange, LIFFE and ISMA. [ 07-22-05 0734ET ]   \par \par \uc2 1089   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020050722e17m00010\par }\pag