\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Singapore sticks to  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  policy after yuan move  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Sebastian Tong  \par \uc2 526 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juli 2005\par 14:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, background)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, July 21 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS), the city-state's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said on Thursday it will maintain its policy of a gradual and modest strengthening of the Singapore dollar following the revaluation of the Chinese yuan.  \par \par \uc2 "Our current exchange rate policy stance remains appropriate. The fundamentals of the economy have not changed, nor have the factors influencing the growth prospects for the Singapore economy," the MAS said in a statement.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS said it will continue to maintain its policy of a "modest and gradual appreciation" of the Singapore dollar policy band as announced in April.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> strengthened by about two percent to 1.6498 per U.S. dollar, a two-month high. That compared with 1.6804 in late trade on Thursday, before news of the revaluation, and a close on Wednesday of 1.6908.  \par \par \uc2 After hitting the intraday high, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pulled back to 1.6574 within half an hour after the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 After the MAS statement, the Singapore dollar eased further to 1.6580.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS conducts monetary policy by steering the Singapore dollar in a band against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than setting an interest rate as many central banks do.  \par \par \uc2 The band and the basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are kept secret.  \par \par \uc2 EYES ON RINGGIT  \par \par \uc2 "We're hearing that the MAS wants to cool the market a bit... maybe they are not happy with the pace of the move and may want to cool the market down," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer who declined to be identified.  \par \par \uc2 A dealer with a Singapore bank said the Singapore dollar's direction in the coming days would depend to a large extent on the movement of the Malaysian ringgit <MYR=>.  \par \par \uc2 Soon after China said it had let its yuan <CNY=CFXS> appreciate, Malaysia announced it had also dropped a policy of fixing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the U.S. dollar in favour of a managed float of the ringgit.  \par \par \uc2 The ringgit had been pegged at 3.8 to the dollar since September 1998 when it was imposed to help Malaysia cope with the Asian financial crisis.  \par \par \uc2 "At this point we are at the stronger side of the band, but where it heads to after this depends on the dollar-ringgit level," said a dealer with a Singapore bank.  \par \par \uc2 Malaysia is Singapore's top trading partner, followed by the European Union, the United States and \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The last time the MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in the fourth quarter of 2004 and in early 2005, when speculation of China and Malaysia changing their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime drove the Singapore dollar higher.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS is due to present its annual report on Friday morning. (Additional reporting by Fayen Wong, Mia Shanley and Vidya Ranganathan) (Writing by Geert De Clercq; mia.shanley@reuters.com; Reuters Messaging: mia.shanley.reuters.com@reuters.net; +65-6870-3305))  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-SINGAPORE-DOLLAR-MAS (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|