\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-EU troubles might temper c.banks appetite for euro  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Natsuko Waki  \par \uc2 807 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 2005\par 12:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, June 17 (Reuters) - The euro may lose its appeal for global central banks seeking to diversify out of the dollar for higher returns as the euro zone faces the spectre of political turmoil and relatively low interest rates. Since its birth in 1999, the euro has lured many central banks, especially in Asia and the Middle East, looking to diversify out of the falling dollar and boost returns.  \par \par \uc2 But returns on euro zone deposits have fallen below those in the United States since December and the Federal Reserve's rate hike campaign this year is set to widen rate differentials.  \par \par \uc2 Since French and Dutch voters rejected a new EU constitution, prospects for political and monetary integration are looking shaky. A Reuters poll of 58 strategists earlier this month gave a 5 percent chance of an EMU collapse in the next 15 years.  \par \par \uc2 All of this could cause many central banks to have second thoughts about accumulating euro reserves.  \par \par \uc2 "Reserve accumulation is akin to accumulating frequent flyer miles: if you suspect an airline may go out of business, it is rational to stop accumulating miles with them," said Stephen Jen, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley.  \par \par \uc2 "The mere risk of a break-up, however remote, may alter the thinking of some central banks."  \par \par \uc2 The euro has fallen 10 percent against the dollar this year, hitting $1.2014 <EUR=> this week, its lowest in more than 9 months.  \par \par \uc2 "There is a very small probability of a catastrophic event but nevertheless you do have to price that," said Steven Pearson, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HBOS Treasury Services.  \par \par \uc2 European policymakers have cheered the fall in the euro, seeing the weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's potential to boost exports and bolster the sluggish economy.  \par \par \uc2 "The issue with European politics at the moment is that a weak euro policy is emerging. This is one factor reserve managers will bear in mind," Pearson said.  \par \par \uc2 The International Monetary Fund's data show the euro's share in official \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose to 19.7 percent in 2003 from 13.5 percent in 1999, while demand for the dollar was stable around 64 percent. Data for 2004 are due later this year.  \par \par \uc2 DIMINISHING RETURNS  \par \par \uc2 Central banks make up an important sector in the $1.9-trillion-a-day \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as total reserves are expected to rise towards $5 trillion by 2008 from around $4 trillion now.  \par \par \uc2 "As portfolio managers, by being long on euro they made a mistake in the past 3-5 months as the euro went down," said Richard Werner, chief investment advisor at hedge fund Profitfundcom.  \par \par \uc2 "To that extent they probably regret the decision to increase euro exposure and some may react by reducing it."  \par \par \uc2 In a recent study of reserves in 100 countries over the period 1990-2004, the IMF said the return on reserves in most countries had peaked in recent years, partly due to the dollar's fall.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF estimates most countries made money on their reserves in 1990-2001, with a median net benefit of holding reserves at 1.1 percent of economic output. But they have lost money since, with the net cost at 0.4 percent of GDP in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 INNOVATIVE MANAGEMENT  \par \par \uc2 Some Asian central banks are coming under political pressure for better management of the reserves, swollen by massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the export-damaging strength of local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "If you can no longer be sure that the euro is going to appreciate both against the dollar and your domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro is going to become much less attractive as a diversifier," Pearson said.  \par \par \uc2 "If Asia were to move to a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  system, emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would unlikely appreciate both against the dollar and the euro."  \par \par \uc2 South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said this week it would lend $5 billion of its \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the world's fourth-largest. It said its net loss this year would more than triple to nearly $500 million, due to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  costs.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has used nearly $60 billion of its \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the world's second largest, to recapitalise state-owned banks, while India plans to use the reserves to upgrade infrastructure.  \par \par \uc2 But politics still play a large part in managing reserves.  \par \par \uc2 Japan, with the world's biggest reserves of more than $800 billion, has repeatedly said it has no intention of changing the composition of its dollar-dominated \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.  \par \par \uc2 "Maybe a third of central banks' thinking is influenced by the portfolio aspects," Werner said.  \par \par \uc2 "If some countries decide they should be totally aligned with the U.S. no matter what, for political reasons, then they will hold onto dollars."  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-EURO-RESERVES (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|ABX|BNX|FUN|J|Z|NAT|MD|SNI|SNS  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050617e16h000u2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
