\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan Resolved To Curb Yen Gains On Yuan Reform  \b0\par\par\uc2 673 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 2005\par 04:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Updates with details, background)  \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki told U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow Friday that the yen has no reason to rise on a Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy change, signaling Tokyo's strong will to prevent such yen gains.  \par \par \uc2 "We don't agree with the market view that the yen will move in the same direction as the Chinese yuan" if Beijing's reform of its fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime causes the Chinese unit to appreciate, Tanigaki told Snow in a bilateral talk before the Group of Eight finance ministers' meeting in London, according to a Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki's remarks are in line with Japan's long-stated official stance. But it was the first time for him to mouth it explicitly to the top U.S. Treasury official in such a high-profile event. That suggests that Tokyo is determined to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to curb any yen rises that analysts say may come if China loosens the yuan from the current pegged level of around CNY8.28 to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki's comments may also damp expectations that recently intensifying calls from U.S. lawmakers against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Asian countries will prompt Tokyo to indefinitely extend its more than a year of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inaction.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -policy makers have been unhappy with speculative yen-buying against the dollar that has happened whenever rumors of an imminent yuan revaluation arose.  \par \par \uc2 Traders purchase the yen and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  partly on the view that a stronger yuan will boost sales of Asian products by making them more competitive relative to Chinese goods.  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo officials have been renouncing the reasoning, saying Japan and China don't compete much in the same export categories. They've also maintained that it's the economic fundamentals of Japan and the U.S. that should determine the dollar-yen exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 Up To China To Decide When To Begin Yuan Reform  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki also told Japanese reporters Friday that China should determine when to change its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, indicating Tokyo's reluctance to join in the U.S. clamor for immediate moves by Beijing.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. over the past few months has made ever-stronger calls on China to let the yuan rise from its current pegged rate, saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is undervalued and therefore subsidizing Chinese exports. But Chinese officials have indicated that they won't bow to international pressure and will allow greater flexibility in the yuan in their own time.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki did say that China will benefit from an early \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -policy reform because a more freely traded yuan will help maintain price stability in the mainland and therefore support its economic growth.  \par \par \uc2 "But China should decide on its own on when and how it will begin (the yuan reform)," Tanigaki said.  \par \par \uc2 His relatively softened approach to the yuan may add to speculation that Tokyo doesn't want to shackle its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy by pressing \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  less to allow more flexibility in the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 Japan right now has few reasons to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the dollar trading around Y108.50, well above this year's low so far of Y101.68. But Tokyo in the past aggressively tried to prop up a falling dollar-yen exchange rate, which troubles Japan's export-driven economy by making Japanese products costlier abroad.  \par \par \uc2 Some analysts suspect that Tokyo's non-confrontational stance may stem from a desire to prevent a worsening of already strained relations with China.  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo wants to ensure that China won't hamper Japan's bid for a permanent seat on the U.N. Security Council, they say. Japan also needs Beijing's help in persuading North Korea to stop preparing tests of nuclear bombs and to provide more information about Japanese citizens kidnapped by North Korea decades ago, they say.  \par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com [ 11-06-05 0232GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 70694  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050611e16b0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
