\par\par \b \uc2 TABLE-Asia ex-China reserves dip $4.6 bln in May   \b0\par\par\uc2 680 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 2005\par 11:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, June 9 (Reuters) - Asian holdings of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves excluding China fell $4.6 billion in May, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  figures show, a fall which \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said reflected a rally in the dollar against the euro <EUR=>.   \par \par \uc2 Including China's holdings as at the end of March -- it has not released figures for April and May -- Asian central banks hold $2.53 trillion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts expect the net change in Asian reserves during May would be positive if China's reserves were included, suspecting that the speculation of a yuan revaluation led to inflows of foreign money into the country in April and May.   \par \par \uc2 They said the fall in reserves for other economies was mostly due to the U.S. dollar's gains against the euro and other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in May, driven by a strengthening U.S. economy, the euro zone's economic woes and uncertainty that culminated in French and Dutch voters rejecting a new EU constitution.   \par \par \uc2 The euro fell almost 4.5 percent against the dollar in May. With an estimated 20 percent share of world reserves, the euro's fall would have caused revaluation losses for reserve-holders.   \par \par \uc2 Japan, Indonesia and India suffered the biggest falls in reserves in dollar terms, each losing over $1 billion in May.   \par \par \uc2 The region's reserves have grown by $83.5 billion so far in 2005. The largest foreign flows have been into \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Malaysia, South Korea and Taiwan, although dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by central banks has been far lower than in 2004, analysts say.   \par \par \uc2 Asia ex-Japan's reserves increased by $173 billion in the last quarter of 2004, which analysts said was a record quarterly jump. China's reserves alone rose over $95 billion in the final quarter of 2004 to a record $609.9 billion.   \par \par \uc2 Amidst the euro's descent and the steep rise in U.S. Treasury prices since March, there has been renewed talk about whether Asian central banks are diversifying their dollar holdings.   \par \par \uc2 Over 60 percent of global \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves were held in dollar assets at the end of 2003, IMF figures show.   \par \par \uc2 Some traders believe the euro's decline has been exacerbated by talk Asian central banks may be less inclined to buy the weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Others felt this was an opportune time for euro-buying by the central banks.   \par \par \uc2 "What would make logical sense in terms of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  allocation is to be buying the euro when it comes off like this," said James Malcolm, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Deutsche Bank in Singapore.   \par \par \uc2 "But that is not the way it seems to work in practice."   \par \par \uc2 Besides fitting in with their longer term investment mandates, such \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying could help restrain the volatility in the euro and in U.S. Treasuries, he said. Latest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve holdings:   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par             Reserves      As at      end-2004       pct\par                $ bln                    $ bln    change\par  Japan           842.47     May 31        844.54     -0.25\par  China*          659.10     Mar 31        609.90     +8.07\par  Taiwan          253.17     May 31        241.70     +4.75\par  South Korea     206.10     May 31        199.07     +3.53\par  India           139.83     May 27        131.18     +6.59\par  Hong Kong       122.40     May 31        123.60     -0.97\par  Singapore       116.00     May 31        112.80     +2.84\par  Malaysia         74.90     May 31         66.70    +12.29\par  Thailand         48.70     May 27         49.80     -2.21\par  Indonesia        34.61     May 31         36.32     -4.71\par  Philippines      17.34     May 31         16.03     +8.17\par  Pakistan         12.71     May 21         11.96     +6.27\par  Bangladesh        3.01     May 31          3.22     -6.52\par  TOTAL         2,530.34                 2,446.82     +3.41\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 *In December 2003, China used $45 billion of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves -- about 10 percent of its holdings at that time -- to bail out two banks. Those funds are not included in this figure. Sources: central banks.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-ASIA-RESERVES|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|D|Z|SI|SNS|SNI|J|IF|PHD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050609e169000sm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
