\par\par \b \uc2 AWSJ(6/8) Pressure Over Yuan May Forestall Action  \b0\par\par\uc2 895 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juni 2005\par 22:05\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2005, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 Jun 07, 2005 (ODC via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 \f2 (From THE ASIAN WALL STREET JOURNAL)\par    By Andrew Browne in Beijing and Greg Ip in Washington\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Zhou Xiaochuan warned that mounting political pressure on Beijing to revalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and overblown expectations about how such a move may help correct imbalances in the world economy, are making it more difficult for China to take action.  \par \par \uc2 But his U.S. counterpart, Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan, said he is confident that China would make a move "reasonably soon," since it was in the country's own best interests. The two bank officials were speaking to a gathering of world bankers yesterday in Beijing.  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Greenspan, talking via satellite linkup, also addressed U.S. financial issues, saying that a puzzling decline in long-term interest rates could signal economic weakness ahead. But he also noted that they aren't as reliable a signal of such weakness as in the past. He expressed concern that hedge funds, taking ever-greater risks to find higher yields, could run into trouble since there were few easy ways left to boost investment returns.  \par \par \uc2 China has come under increasing pressure from its trade partners to revalue the yuan. U.S. and European manufacturers complain that an overly cheap yuan gives Chinese companies an unfair advantage by making their exports more competitive. U.S. President George W. Bush's administration is trying to stave off growing protectionist pressure in congress by pushing China for a substantial appreciation of the yuan, which is pegged at 8.28 yuan to the dollar. The U.S. Treasury has said China risks trade sanctions unless it eases the peg within six months.  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Zhou, an English-speaking technocrat, is widely believed to advocate a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and greater use of other market mechanisms, such as interest rates, to iron out the boom-bust cycles that plague the Chinese economy. But some Chinese officials fear that if China abandons the peg, it would be pulling up an anchor that has held the economy steady for more than a decade.  \par \par \uc2 Yesterday, Mr. Zhou touched upon some of those concerns, telling bankers at the International Monetary Conference that China is concerned about how any yuan appreciation could impact domestic jobs and growth. And while China remains committed to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  overhauls, he said, China's banks need more time to prepare.  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Zhou suggested that politically inspired pressure on China to revalue is counterproductive. "This is not a favorable environment to promote reform," he said. He said that China's global trade accounts for a small part of the world total, and that the rest of the world expects too much from a yuan appreciation. "It's too heavy on our shoulders," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Greenspan acknowledged that a stronger yuan doesn't mean the overall U.S. deficit "will shrink very much, if at all," since U.S. importers simply would turn to other countries offering cheap goods. But he said a stronger yuan may help reduce the U.S. bilateral trade deficit with \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He also argued that Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the yuan from rising couldn't continue indefinitely. A more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  "is very much to the advantage of China," he said.  \par \par \uc2 European officials also have been pressuring China to allow the yuan to appreciate. But China is only part of Europe's economic problem. The 12-nation euro zone's economic recovery is faltering due to weak domestic demand, and the International Monetary Fund and politicians are increasing their calls for an interest-rate cut by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Economists are deeply divided over whether Beijing would revalue anytime soon. James McCormack, senior director at agency Fitch Ratings, said yesterday that he doesn't believe Beijing would do so and risk hurting exports. Exports have become an important growth engine as Chinese authorities crack down on overheated investment. Economic growth "comes above all else," Mr. McCormack said. "They'll try to ride out trade disputes."  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Greenspan, offering his thoughts on falling long-term interest rates, said a prominent hypothesis for the decline is that "the markets are signaling economic weakness. This is certainly a credible notion." He then cast doubt on it by noting that "periodic signs of buoyancy" in some parts of the world haven't stopped the decline.  \par \par \uc2 Since June 2004, the Fed has raised its short-term rate target to 3% from 1% and has signaled plans to raise it further, while the 10-year Treasury bond yield has fallen to less than 4% from 4.7%. That sort of decline in long-term rates "is clearly without recent precedent," Mr. Greenspan said.  \par \par \uc2 A weakening economy often brings about lower long-term interest rates because it reduces inflationary pressure and demand for credit and leads to inferior returns on competing investments such as stocks. Historically, a decline in bond yields to, or below, the short-term interest rate set by the Fed -- called an "inverted yield curve" -- has been a precursor of economic slowdown or recession. Since the spread between short- and long-term rates has narrowed to less than one percentage point from almost three percentage points in the past year, some analysts predict the yield curve will invert.  \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires  \par \par \uc2 06-07-05 1731ET  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020050608e167000mp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
