\par\par \b \uc2 DJ HKMA To Hold News Conference On  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Board -2-  \b0\par\par\uc2 197 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Mai 2005\par 12:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2005, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 On Saturday, the South China Morning Post quoted a Hong Kong government adviser saying that a formal limit to how far the Hong Kong dollar can gain against its U.S. counterpart is being considered, although HK$7.8 will be kept as the fixed peg for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The HKMA doesn't allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise far beyond HK$7.8 to the U.S. dollar, but there is no set level at which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  will allow the yuan to rise has recently put upward pressure on the Hong Kong dollar.  \par \par \uc2 John Greenwood, architect of the peg and a member of the HKMA's committee on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  is looking at fine tuning the system because speculative inflows are forcing the territory's interest rates to diverge from U.S. rates, despite the peg to the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 A ceiling for the Hong Kong dollar is seen as one option, reported the SCMP. -By Hong Kong Bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; djnews.hongkong@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by David Riordan  \par \par \uc2 -0-  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020050518e15i0066a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
