\par\par \b \uc2 DJ US Treasury: China Likely To Violate  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Law If No Change -2-  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Price Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 791 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 2005\par 21:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2005, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--The U.S. Treasury on Tuesday said no major U.S. trading partners now qualify for penalties in retaliation for manipulating global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to boost exports, but warned China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg is likely to run afoul of U.S. law soon.  \par \par \uc2 'The report finds that no major trading partner of the U.S. met the technical requirements for designation under the Ombibus Trade and Competitiveness Act of 1988 during the second half of 2004,' Treasury said in a semiannual report on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, Treasury said China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies 'are highly distortionary and pose a risk to China's economy, its trading partners and global economic growth.'  \par \par \uc2 'If current trends continue without substantial alteration, China's policies will likely meet the technical requirements of the statute for designation,' Treasury said.  \par \par \uc2 The report, originally due April 15, has been required by Congress since 1988. A largely backward-looking review of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market developments in major trading partners, the review has gained prominence as U.S. manufacturers, and the members of Congress who represent them, complain China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg undervalues the Chinese yuan and unfairly cheapens its exports.  \par \par \uc2 The Chinese government tightly manages its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  via \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a system of capital controls, effectively pegging it at CNY8.28 to the U.S. dollar. For almost two years, the Bush administration has publicly urged China to allow \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to have more influence on the value of the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 Although Chinese officials have said repeatedly they intend to adopt a flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they have also said it won't happen until the ailing financial system can handle the adjustment. The delay has provoked impatience from the U.S. Congress, where various legislative proposals would impose trade sanctions on Chinese imports in retaliation for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg.  \par \par \uc2 During the second half of 2004, China's economy slowed thanks to a series of administrative measures to curb lending and raise domestic interest rates slightly. Still, economic data this year suggest that 'China's growth rate and inflation risks are rebounding,' the Treasury said.  \par \par \uc2 The Treasury argued that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg is a growing threat to China's own economic stability. Defending the peg makes it more difficult for the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  (PBOC) to fight inflation. Massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  required to prevent the yuan from appreciating pumps up the money supply and forces the PBOC to sell ever-growing amounts of bonds to soak up liquidity. The desire to discourage new capital inflows prevents the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from raising interest rates to a level required to fight inflation in the rapidly growing economy.  \par \par \uc2 Easy credit also raises the risk that new investment may not be productive, creating a new generation of non-performing loans in the banking system, the Treasury said.  \par \par \uc2 Although China has held the world's largest bilateral trade surplus with the U.S. for several years, its overall global trade has been close to balance. That trend appears to be changing to one of growing trade surpluses, the Treasury noted. In 2004, China's current account surplus rose sharply to $68.7 billion, equal to 4.2% of the economy, compared with $45.9 billion in 2003, and $17.4 billion in 2001.  \par \par \uc2 'In the context of an economy with large and dependable capital inflows, large prospects for productivity gains, and unsustainably high rates of investment, China's current account surplus is large,' the Treasury said.  \par \par \uc2 The large and, for many economists, unsustainable U.S. current account deficit is the flip side of large current account surpluses in major trading partners, the department said. Achieving more balanced trade around the world will require the U.S. to increase savings as well as more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility in Asia and faster growth in Europe and Japan, the Treasury said.  \par \par \uc2 China's government has made great strides in reforming the economy and preparing for the introduction of greater \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility, the Treasury said. Some of these reforms include development of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, easing capital controls on the amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that can move in and out of the country, development of hedging instruments and improving bank supervision.  \par \par \uc2 'China is now ready to move to a more flexible exchange rate and should move now,' the Treasury said. 'Treasury will monitor progress on China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market developments very closely over the next six months in advance of preparation of the fall report.' The fall report is due Oct. 15. -By Elizabeth Price, 