\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-ANALYSIS-Yuan scare helps markets prepare for revaluation  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Sabyasachi Mitra  \par \uc2 709 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2005\par 17:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Repeats to remove superfluous word in 1st paragraph)  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, May 12 (Reuters) - Recent false alarms over a long-awaited change to China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime may have helped the market prepare for what might happen on the day China actually bites the bullet and revalues the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 Global financial markets have been on tenterhooks and speculation has swirled for months that China is set to reform its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg.  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, investors scrambled to place bets and cover positions when the ruling communist party's mouthpiece, the People's Daily, briefly carried a report about an imminent yuan revaluation.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading volumes on electronic broker EBS rose close to record highs on Wednesday after the report.  \par \par \uc2 EBS said its daily trading volume on Wednesday was over $160 billion, exceeding its average daily volume of $110-120 billion. It also said it had trading periods during the day that were close to EBS records.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen rose against the dollar and the euro and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  registered gains against the greenback after the report.  \par \par \uc2 The yen gave up its gains after the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said there was no change to its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and the report was withdrawn by the newspaper from its Web site.  \par \par \uc2 This was not the first time that markets were rattled by news that China may have pressed the button on yuan reforms.  \par \par \uc2 Last month, the yuan briefly moved to 8.2700 per dollar -- outside its normal range of 8.2760 to 8.2800 per dollar -- after an apparent trading error on the official Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system allowed the yuan to move above its band.  \par \par \uc2 "These incidents can be used to learn how the market would behave when the revaluation eventually happens," said Copenhagen-based Niels From, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Danske Bank.  \par \par \uc2 A research report from HBOS said appreciation of the yuan would be a catalyst for a shift in the burden of dollar adjustment to developing from developed market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The yuan, or renminbi, is pegged in a narrow band around 8.28 per dollar. As the dollar has broadly weakened over the last three and a half years, the falling yuan has made Chinese exports cheaper in global markets.  \par \par \uc2 DRY RUNS?  \par \par \uc2 Marvin Barth of Citigroup says reaction to false alarms over likely yuan policy change shows markets are increasingly prepared for a move by China.  \par \par \uc2 Most analysts say that once Beijing revalues the yuan, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will rally, with the Korean won and the Taiwanese dollar leading the way.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen could also rally initially but the Bank of Japan may step in to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from gaining rapidly.  \par \par \uc2 The HBOS report said commodity bloc \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could trade as emerging Asian proxies and outperform their peers in developed markets vis-a-vis the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 But Bhanu Baweja, Singapore-based strategist at UBS, says once \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  revalues the yuan, Asian policy makers will step up \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  competitive.  \par \par \uc2 A Chinese revaluation would also reduce the need for China to buy dollars to hold the peg, which implies less capital inflow and reduced demand for U.S. Treasuries.  \par \par \uc2 While the markets grapple with what will be the impact of a likely yuan revaluation, some analysts speculate whether the false alarms were attempts by Beijing to test the market.  \par \par \uc2 "The Chinese authorities are playing a cat and mouse game with the markets," said Neil Mackinnon at ECU Group in London.  \par \par \uc2 "It's entirely possible that it was a dry run, designed to put speculative traders off their guard. If that was the intention, it was highly successful," Mackinnon said.  \par \par \uc2 China, the world's third-largest trading nation and seventh biggest economy, often unveils key economic policies without a warning and that is one reason why global markets were rocked by the People's Daily report on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 "The report was quickly quashed by the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  but, one day, one of these stories may well prove correct," Bear Stearns said in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Natsuko Waki and Christina Fincher)  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RPT)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|Z|UKI|NAT|J|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050512e15c001ry\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
