\par\par \b \uc2 DJ INTERVIEW: Ex-IMF Official Urges China To Act Soon On  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By LEIKA KIHARA Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 621 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 2005\par 10:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2005, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--China is in a position to adopt a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime and shouldn't wait too long to move so it can achieve balanced economic growth in the long term, Shigemitsu Sugisaki, former deputy managing director of the International Monetary Fund, said Sunday.  \par \par \uc2 'China has been preparing (to change its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime) for such a long time and it knows what it needs to do now,' Sugisaki said.  \par \par \uc2 'Taking appropriate steps promptly' toward \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility would give China more room in guiding monetary policy and ease speculative capital flows by eliminating unnecessary speculation over the issue, Sugisaki said in an interview with Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 'I'm not saying this to pressure China or to help Japanese exporters,' Sugisaki said. 'It's for China's own sake' to introduce more flexibility in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system soon, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Sugisaki, who oversaw Asian economies during his stint at the IMF from 1997 to 2004, said China and other Asian economies won't be hurt much and won't face financial market instability even if Beijing decides to change its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime by the end of this year.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S., Europe and the IMF have called on China to loosen its tightly controlled yuan peg to the dollar, arguing that China's undervalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps Chinese exports artificially inexpensive.  \par \par \uc2 Chinese officials didn't attend the two-day Group of Seven finance officials' meeting that ended Saturday, but that didn't prevent G7 nations, particularly the U.S., from turning up the heat on China to reform its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow told reporters after the G7 meeting Saturday that China has made a lot of progress modernizing its financial institutions and 'it's time to take the next step and move toward more (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) flexibility.'  \par \par \uc2 The IMF has argued that China should adopt more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility because it would allow its monetary policy to focus on alleviating inflation instead of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Shouldn't Wait Until Bank-Sector Woes Solved  \par \par \uc2 China keeps its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, effectively pegged to the dollar, allowing it to trade within a thin band of 0.3% on either side of 8.277 to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Sugisaki, who is now chairman of the private think tank Sompo Japan Research Institute, said China should first adjust the yuan's exchange rate peg against the dollar and then widen the band, because Beijing won't be able to prevent speculative capital inflows just by expanding the band itself.  \par \par \uc2 Sugisaki shrugged off the view that China should wait until its banking-sector woes are solved in changing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime.  \par \par \uc2 'It's true that bad loans accumulating at Chinese banks are a serious problem,' Sugisaki said. 'But various imbalances within China's economy would continue to grow if it were to wait until its bad-loan problems are fixed,' he said.  \par \par \uc2 The Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, purchasing dollars to prevent the yuan from appreciating. As a result, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves jumped $49.2 billion in the first quarter of this year to a record $659.1 billion.  \par \par \uc2 The rapid pace of growth in foreign reserves is due in large part to speculative capital inflows, a situation that needs to be dealt with soon in order for China to achieve long-term balanced economic growth, Sugisaki said.  \par \par \uc2 'China needs to pursue economic reform in various areas, but it needs to do so quickly,' he said. -By Leika Kihara, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; leika.kihara@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -0-  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020050418e14i00695\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
