\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: G7 Intrigue Fades With Dlr Up, PBOC Gov Out  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,120 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2005\par 23:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Steven Vames \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--There's just a bit more than a week to go before finance ministers from the Group of Seven rich nations meet in Washington, but the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market is finding less and less to get excited about.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 First, there's the dollar itself. With the greenback not far from its best levels of the year and well away from the depths where it sat at the end of last year, global officials are likely more sanguine than they have been at other meetings and heated discussions about the dollar's value probably won't dominate the agenda.  \par \par \uc2 Then there's the fact that the U.S. Treasury said China won't be sending its most senior officials to the meeting. While China's motivation isn't clear, the outcome is: without China's top decision makers present, there will be no significant discussion of the Chinese yuan, one of the most pressing issues in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and global economics these days.  \par \par \uc2 With those factors in place, the G7 meeting will have very little intrigue for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, in contrast with other recent meetings in which the urgency of the dollar's weakness or utterances of officials on China caused markets to gyrate. Even if those meetings themselves didn't yield much new on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  front, the lead-up to them was at least enough to keep toes curled in the market. This time, the meeting is more likely to yield yawns both before and after.  \par \par \uc2 The Treasury Department has said as much, with a spokesman saying earlier this week the U.S. will repeat its strong dollar policy and calls for flexibility, but that he didn't expect \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be a "hot issue."  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Eye On China \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, China's decision not to send its most senior officials has, itself, become a curiosity for the markets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China keeps its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  effectively pegged to the dollar through massive market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which keeps the yuan artificially weak and helps maintain \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's ability to export heavily to the U.S.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The unintended side effect is that China's cheap exports to the U.S. have played a big role in blowing out the U.S. current account deficit, making the U.S. dependent on luring back via investment the money it sends overseas for goods. Worry about the sustainability of that relationship caused the dollar to fall sharply last year, even as U.S. economic growth outpaced many of its developed counterparts.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The official call for China and other Asian nations to liberalize \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policies started in earnest just over a year ago, when at a meeting in Boca Raton, Fla, G7 officials said in a statement that "more flexibility in exchange rates is desirable for major countries...that lack such flexibility." The group has repeated that language at every subsequent meeting.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China, which isn't a member of the G7, has since been invited to and attended the two most recent G7 meetings. At the meeting in early February, it sent Peoples Bank of China Governor Zhou Xiachuan, but this time it's offering PBOC deputy governor Li Ruogu and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  vice minister Li Yong.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the meetings, China's officials indicated a determination to work towards a more flexible exchange rate system, but said they couldn't give a time table for doing so. The latest G7 statement in early February said only that officials had "continued productive talks" with China.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Such talk had lent credibility to many economists' beliefs that China would indeed make some move to revalue its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this year. That has significant implications for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets since many believe a China revaluation would also take some competitive pressure off other Asian export-based economies, such as Japan and Korea, would would then allow their own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to appreciate.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many also believe that if China were to become more flexible, the euro in particular would start to act as less of a pressure relief valve for speculation against the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      Fading Into Background \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 More recently, however, the China issue has faded into the background, partly due to the dollar's broad rebound from the lows it hit at the end of 2004. Since then, the euro has declined about 5.6% versus the dollar and the greenback has gained roughly 5.9% against the yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dashed hopes for action by China in the first quarter have also highlighted the difficulty of predicting such a move, and some baby steps by China, including an interest rate hike and bank reforms, have also fostered a belief that China's timeline might be longer than previously thought.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's decision not to send its top brass to next week's G7 might keep the issue tucked in the background.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "For markets, it's being seen as something that reduces the chances of a move on the yuan," said Jens Nordvig, global markets economist at Goldman Sachs in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some suggest China may simply have nothing to add to what it said after the prior two meetings, and is looking to avoid being an target of international \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  mirth once again.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "China made very clear statements in the fall, and they probably don't have anything to add," said Adam Posen, Senior Fellow at the Institute for International Economics. "Why bother to get yelled at and then have everybody parse everything they say if there is no new message?"  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Others speculate that China's diminished presence at the G7 meeting and the broader IMF and World Bank meeting that the G7 runs alongside of, may be a sort of protest.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's happened the week following the discussions between Europe and the U.S. over the arms embargo," said John Rutledge, chairman of Rutledge Capital an investment and private equity firm in Greenwich, CT, who was an advisor to former President Ronald Reagan and to President George W. Bush. "That's another reason to stump their feet."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If correct, that could further confirm the lack of lines drawn between China's economic and political decision making process. It could also give political issues - namely Taiwan, arms sales, and conspicuous absences of top government officials at global economic forums - a heightened importance in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Steven Vames covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in New York. He has also covered equities in Los Angeles and the Treasurys market and fixed income industry in New York.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -Steven Vames; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-5063; steven.vames@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Laurence Norman contributed to this report) [ 04-06-05 1705ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1141  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020050406e1460008a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
