\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ CSFB Sees Dlr Significantly Undervalued Vs Majors, Euro  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Laurence Norman Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 748 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 10:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2005, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar is significantly undervalued versus major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a fair value basis but remains expensive on a global trade-weighted index, Credit Suisse First Boston (CSF.YY) said in a report Wednesday on long-term \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  valuations.  \par \par \uc2 The annual report also concluded that China's yuan is some 45% below its fair value versus the dollar and said the euro is 'universally very expensive' given long-term economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 CSFB, one of the larger participants in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, said the dollar is 9% undervalued versus the majors, a 'significant' deviation though below the extreme plus or minus 15% deviations the dollar last experienced in 1984, 1995 and 2002. On a global basis, the dollar is 10% more expensive than fair valuation would suggest, largely because of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , many of which are kept directly or indirectly tied to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The bank said that appreciating Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  point to further dollar weakness in early 2005 before the impact of rising U.S. interest rates spark a dollar recovery on a trade-weighted basis from 2006.  \par \par \uc2 CSFB said the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is 20% overvalued versus the dollar and is therefore 'vulnerable to medium-to- longer-term decline.' The bank sees the fair euro value at $1.08 versus the dollar, instead of the $1.3405 at which it was trading Wednesday afternoon. In line with the bank's view that the dollar will start to feel the impact of rate rises a year into the Fed tightening cycle, CSFB sees the euro peaking in 'mid-2005 and declining thereafter.'  \par \par \uc2 The bank also said the euro is 'expensive versus almost all \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and particularly so versus the Japanese yen, Swiss franc and Swedish krona.'  \par \par \uc2 In a separate \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  report earlier Wednesday, CSFB adjusted its euro/dollar forecasts. The bank became more dollar bearish in the short run, seeing the euro at $1.38 in three months from $1.35 previously. However, the bank is more bullish in the medium term, forecasting a year-end euro spot value of $1.25 from $1.30.  \par \par \uc2 The euro has borne the brunt of the dollar's three years of declines. Investors who might have speculated on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength versus the dollar have often bought euros because there is less threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Yuan Very Cheap, But No Big Change Likely  \par \par \uc2 CSFB also estimated that China's yuan which is effectively pegged at CNY8.28 per dollar is now 45% undervalued versus the dollar. However, the bank said that 'any really large-scale adjustment in the exchange rate is unlikely. China's need to maintain a high pace of domestic growth, in order to absorb the explosion in its non-rural labor force, suggests any readjustment will be modest.'  \par \par \uc2 In the earlier report Wednesday, CSFB predicted that not even a modest change to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg will come before May at the earliest. However, some change remains a 'real possibility' over the next year.  \par \par \uc2 In its other conclusions, CSFB said the yen is close to fair value but predicted it will overshoot to 'expensive' levels in 2005 as the trade surplus is not offset by large capital outflows. A change in the way Japan calculates Gross Domestic Product means the fair dollar-yen rate is now Y101, down from Y93 in 2004, according to CSFB.The dollar was fetching Y103.81 Wednesday afternoon. Other overvalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a trade-weighted basis included the Australian dollar, sterling, Norway's krone and - most expensive of all - the New Zealand dollar. Other than the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Sweden's krona and Mexico's peso were significantly undervalued. Korea's won, the Swiss franc and Canada's dollar were close to fair value.  \par \par \uc2 Versus the U.S. dollar, the most overvalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the Czech koruna, followed by the Polish zloty.  \par \par \uc2 CSFB's exchange rate fair value model uses a data-based model that seeks to come up with a value 'driven by long-term fundamental relationships ... rather than simply a description of the history of one exchange rate.'  \par \par \uc2 Among the variables factored into the model are 'relative interest rates, productivity and external balances' as well as the purchasing power parity measures that analysts often take as a rough fair value guideline.  \par \par \uc2 -Laurence Norman; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; laurence.norman@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020050310e13a008kf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
