\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\uc2  in 2004 hit $195 bln.  \b0\par\par\uc2 537 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 05:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, March 1 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday it spent 1.61 trillion yuan ($195 billion) buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2004 to maintain the yuan peg against the dollar, a rise of 40 percent over 2003.  \par \par \uc2 As a result of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was forced to drain a net 669 billion yuan from the banking system via open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2004 - more than double the 282 billion yuan drained in 2003.  \par \par \uc2 "The huge amount of base money in the market ... has made the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  face strong pressure to manage money flow and tweak base money supply," the People's Bank of China said in a report posted on its Web site ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.pbc.gov.cn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.pbc.gov.cn }}}\uc2 ).  \par \par \uc2 China has managed the yuan in a razor-thin range of 8.276 to 8.28 against the dollar since the Asian financial crisis in 1997/1998, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is under pressure to rise because of heavy speculative and investment inflows.  \par \par \uc2 Beijing is under pressure from the United States and other developed economies to let the yuan appreciate. They say the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is undervalued, giving Chinese exporters an unfair advantage in world export markets.  \par \par \uc2 Turnover on the China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System, which trades U.S. dollars, the Japanese yen, the euro and Hong Kong dollars against the yuan, jumped 38 percent to a record $209.04 billion in 2004 - the highest since the market was established a decade ago, exchange data showed.  \par \par \uc2 Strong trade surpluses and a heavy inflow of foreign investment has left the market flush with \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past five years and market players say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was virtually the only buyer of surplus hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market.  \par \par \uc2 The nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves hit a record $610 billion at the end of 2004, up $206.7 billion from a year earlier, official data showed.  \par \par \uc2 In addition, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is coping with billions of dollars in hot money flowing into China betting on an appreciation of the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 China has resisted foreign pressure to free up the yuan but has promised to make the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more flexible over time through gradual reform.  \par \par \uc2 Keen to relieve pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , China will cut its growing balance of payments surplus by letting more foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  leave the country, state media cited the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief as saying on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say Beijing is more concerned with the country's overall macroeconomic health than the cost of offsetting the impact of surging foreign reserves.  \par \par \uc2 Beijing made open-market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  an increasingly important tool in 2004 to tweak the supply of funds in the banking system, which had fuelled lending and stoked inflationary fears in the world's seventh-biggest economy.  \par \par \uc2 Total turnover in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's open-market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  jumped to 1.997 trillion yuan in 2004, it said in the annual report, up from 1.209 trillion yuan in 2003 and 364 billion yuan in 2002.  \par \par \uc2 "Along with the development of the open-market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , interest rates on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills have become increasingly benchmarks for China's money market," it said. ($1=8.28 Yuan).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050301e131004en\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
