\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Reserve Talk May Continue To Haunt Dollar  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,016 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Februar 2005\par 23:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Laurence Norman \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Warning to all holders of dollar assets: Don't mention reserve diversification.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The dollar slid overnight after the Bank of Korea said in a document intended for a subcommittee of the National Assembly that it will diversify its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to expand its purchases of high-yielding non-government debt and include more \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The details of what the BOK said were murky and analysts noted the bank has made similar comments in the past. Yet that didn't prevent the dollar registering it largest drop versus the euro in six months and falling to a multiyear low versus the Korean won.  \par \par \uc2 The overnight move wasn't the only recent sign of traders' extreme sensitivity to the reserve issue. The dollar sunk to a then-record low versus the euro on Nov. 23, after Russia said it would review the amount of euro reserves it held. The selloff came even though Russia had been gradually raising the ratio of euro-denominated reserves for some time.  \par \par \uc2 A couple of weeks later, the dollar enjoyed one of the few happy days it had in late 2004, climbing after Japanese Finance Ministry Sadakazu Tanigaki made the apparently obvious statement that reserve diversification required "very careful" discussion.  \par \par \uc2 So why is the dollar so touchy? Part of the answer is timing. Coming during a third year of dollar declines, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp retreat late last year made it vulnerable to anything that added to the bearish mood. It's perhaps no coincidence that the BOK's musings on reserves sparked such a big selloff, just days after the market mood turned against the dollar's prospects again, following an early 2005 dollar rebound.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par        Central Banks Become Central \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bigger issue is that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar reserves and holdings of Treasurys have soared in recent years, as authorities across the world, particularly in Asia, bought dollars to stem \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreign central banks held some $1.9 trillion in Treasurys alone, at the end of 2004, according to the U.S. Treasury. That's up from $1.5 trillion a year earlier and $1.2 trillion at 2003's close. Meanwhile, China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves rose 51.3% in 2004 to more than $600 billion. South Korea's gained over 25% to nearly $200 billion. Then, there's Japan, which has a record $845 billion sitting in the Bank of Japan's coffers.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With such huge dollar holdings, spot market movements could swing wildly if a couple of central banks decided to quickly pare back their ratio of dollar reserves. And if South Korea, for example, decided to dump the U.S. Treasurys it holds - about $70 billion - bond markets could be jolted just as hard as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It's not only the direct effect of a reduction in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar-asset holdings the market is nervous about, said Alan Ruskin, director of research at New York-based advisory service 4Cast. The concern about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  plans links directly into the market's broader fears over whether the U.S. can continue funding its large current account deficit. Concerns about the deficit have been a key factor in the dollar's recent declines.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What's more, Ruskin said, any official pronouncements on dollar holdings could have a major impact on the decision of overseas private investors. The December Treasury International Capital Systems data showed purchases of U.S. assets by the foreign private sector played a much larger role in funding the current account deficit. If these investors come to believe central banks are tired of absorbing losses from dollar declines, private buying could also plummet.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It can become something of a vicious cycle," Ruskin said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Watch Japan \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, analysts said there are some very good reasons for central banks not to sell dollars, even if they're convinced the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will continue to drop. Most central banks are more concerned with market stability than making a quick buck from the dollar's decline. A massive decline in the dollar and a crisis in the Treasury market will hardly benefit Japanese exporters or Korean banks.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What's more, central banks know that since they can't dump all their dollars in one fell-swoop, diversifying reserves can easily be counter-productive. If their selling pushes the dollar lower, it will reduce the worth of the rest of their assets even more.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As a result, Ruskin said while there did seem to have been a pickup in reserve diversification in recent months, the process still seemed "glacial."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There are red flags out there which should stop investors being complacent about central banks decisions.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One is Japan. Noting that Japan's holdings of Treasurys fell in late 2004 for the first time in two years, Ashraf Laidi, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst at MG Financial Group, predicted that a shift away from dollar assets by the Bank of Japan is "inevitable in order to avoid massive losses in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's dollar portfolios."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Laidi calculated that if the dollar falls to Y100 from its current Y104.07, it would inflict a $28 billion hole in Japan's U.S. Treasurys portfolio. If the market comes to believe that Japan, it could hit the dollar hard.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The other issue surrounds what happens if China revalues the yuan versus the dollar. A move from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would relax the pressure on other Asian countries to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to limit their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's appreciation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That could mean a substantial drop in dollar demand at a time when central banks were already planning to reduce their dollar holdings. If the dollar falls as a result, it may just persuade central banks to accelerate the pace at which they throw out the greenbacks.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Laurence Norman covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in New York. He has previously covered the Argentine economy for Dow Jones in Buenos Aires.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Laurence Norman, Dow Jones Newswires, 1-201-938-2096; laurence.norman@dowjones.com [ 02-22-05 1745ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020050222e12m00037\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
