\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Feb 21  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,309 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Februar 2005\par 10:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: A protection fund for securities investors being planned in China may give the domestic market a modest dose of confidence in the short term, but is unlikely to address key investor worries hampering the nation's bourses, analysts said Monday. (UPDATE to run by 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Japanese officials have been warning market players that Tokyo is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the yen rises on a \b \highlight22\uc2 China currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy change, keeping traders on their toes despite Japan's absence from the market recently. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Japan Aims To Preempt Yen Rise On Yuan Shift," ran at 0332 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: Robust growth in Indian industry and a heightened sense of competition with China may persuade the Congress-led government to sidestep political considerations and push ahead with structural reforms to boost the sector at the Feb. 28 annual budget. ("=INDIA BUDGET: Industrial Revival May Prompt Infras Boost" to run by 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: UK Chancellor of the Exchequer Gordon Brown is in Beijing and expected to meet with Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao late Monday. (Story possible after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's producer price index rose 5.8% year on year in January, a slowdown from December's increase, the National Bureau of Statistics reported Monday. ("China Jan PPI Rises 5.8% On Year; Growth Slows From Dec," first take ran at 0225 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Hong Kong's jobless rate edged down to 6.4% in the three-month period ended January, supported by a strong local economy, official statistics issued Monday show. (DATA VIEW to run by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Relaxed lending controls could help promote nonbank lending, providing a source of funding for small- and medium-sized firms and individual borrowers, a senior China \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said. ("China Ctrl Bk Wu: Relaxed Controls To Aid Nonbank Lending," first take ran at 0422 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's financial institutions lent 6.3% more last year than in 2003, but the pace in loans growth slowed sharply, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Monday. ("S Korea Fincl Institution Loans Up 6.3% '04 Vs 12.9% '03," ran at 0300 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Bank of China, one of China's four biggest lenders, said Monday it adjusted the size of its subordinated bond tranches due to strong demand. ("Bank Of China Adjusts Sub-bond Tranches On Strong Demand," ran at 0640 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan's balance of payments surplus declined to US$2.9 billion in the fourth quarter of 2004, from a surplus of US$12.43 billion in the year-earlier period, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Monday. ("Taiwan's 4Q Balance Of Payments Surplus US$2.9 Bln," first take ran at 0839 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's National Development and Reform Commission, the country's top economic planning agency, issued detailed rules on approving investments in mobile phone equipment manufacturing. (Story to run by around 1045 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thailand's Government Pension Fund said Monday that it plans to boost its investment in foreign securities to THB13 billion-THB14 billion this year to better diversify its risks. ("Thai Pension Fund To Raise Foreign Sec Invest To THB14B," ran at 0748 GMT)  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Option Views: The most notable thing about this week's relative value tables, which rank the major option markets by their one-year Z-scores, is the fact that only two - three-month and six-month USD/CAD - of the 40 different at-the-money implied vols we track are trading above their one-year means. That seems to suggest growing complacency in the market for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk. As always, we favor taking the other side of this view. ("\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Option Views: Volatility Mkts Look A Bit Complacent", ran at 0200 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Foreign players increased their net purchases of Japanese bonds in January, while major Japanese banks remained net sellers of the bonds for the seventh consecutive month, data released by the Japan Securities Dealers Association showed Monday. ("Foreigners Increase Net Japan Bond Purchases In Jan -JSDA," ran at 0843 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: A World Bank economist said Monday that the success of the current round of global trade talks could help lift hundreds of millions of China's farmers out of poverty - but Chinese officials sounded pessimistic about the prospect of real progress. (Story to run by 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: The New Zealand median house price rose to a new record in January, but fewer houses were sold and they took longer to sell, suggesting caution among buyers and sellers, according to data released Monday. ("NZ January House Prices Rise To New Record; Fewer Sold" ran at 1843 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HANOI: Vietnam's first-quarter exports are expected to rise 25% on year to $6.9 billion, helped by an increase in domestic companies' shipments, a Trade Ministry report said Monday. ("Vietnam Forecasts 1Q Exports +25% To $6.9B; Imports $7.6B," ran at 0352 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Sankyo Co. and Daiichi Pharmaceutical Co. each said Monday that tying up with another company is a strategic option, adding credence to a local media report that they plan to merge to create the second biggest drug company in Japan. ("=Sankyo, Daiichi Pharm Open To Ties, Signaling More M&A," ran at 0915 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Emboldened by a profit-boosting acquisition in New Zealand, news company John Fairfax Holdings Ltd. is ready to swoop on one of Australia's major television networks. ("=2nd UPDATE: Fairfax 1H Net Dn 18%, TV Networks In Sights," ran at 0528 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Nissan Motor Co. said Monday that it has appointed Senior Vice President Toshiyuki Shiga as its Chief Operating Officer, effective April 1. ("Nissan Motor Appoints Senior VP Toshiyuki Shiga As COO," first take ran at 0908 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Toshiba Corp. said Monday it will speed up its investment schedule for large-capacity memory chip production to prepare for an expected rise in demand as the market for electronic appliances expands. ("Toshiba To Bring Forward Capex Budget For Chip Plant," ran at 0250 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Monaco-based Sovereign Asset Management said Monday that it has no intention of replacing the current management at both LG Electronics Co. and LG Corp. and supports the current management team. ("Sovereign Asset Mgmt: Support LG's Current Management," ran at 0143 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BURLEIGH HEADS, Australia: Australian surfwear company Billabong International Ltd. said Monday fiscal first-half net profit rose 73% to A$70.1 million, from A$40.6 million in the same period a year ago. ("Australia's Billabong 1H Net Surges 73% To A$70.1 Mln," first take ran at 2234 GMT; see also "=INTERVIEW: Australia Billabong Rides Perfect Wave In 1H," at 0629 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Evans & Tate Ltd., a small Australian winemaker, said Monday it isn't opposed to the proposed A$3.1 billion tie-up of much bigger local rivals Foster's Group Ltd. and Southcorp Ltd. In fact, the Western Australian maker of brands such as Evans & Tate and Barramundi said a combination of Foster's and Southcorp has the potential to benefit the company's sales and put it on the radar of more investors. ("=INTERVIEW: Australia's Evans & Tate OK On Foster's Bid," ran at 0140 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Venture Ltd., Singapore's largest contract manufacture of electronics goods such as printers for Hewlett-Packard, to report full-year earnings. (Story to run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: With a foothold in China, Axa Asia Pacific Holdings Ltd. is eyeing India's funds and life insurance market, confirming talks are underway to start a joint-venture there as part of a planned A$300 million multi-year regional expansion. The comments came as Axa Asia Pacific Monday reported a 42% drop in calendar 2004 profit to A$540 million from A$926 million a year earlier when gains were boosted by the sale of a health insurance business. ("=UPDATE: After China, Axa Asia Eyes India Joint Venture," ran at 0741 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Compal Communications Inc. expects its handset shipments to rise sharply to 20 million units this year from 8.6 million units in 2004, a senior company official said. ("=INTERVIEW:Taiwan Compal Commun Sees Strong Growth In '05," ran at 0257 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taishin Financial Holding Co. said Monday its net profit in 2005 will likely come in at NT$13.5 billion, or NT$3.19 a share. ("Taiwan's Taishin Fincl Expects 2005 Net NT$13.5 Bln," ran at 0612 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Citizen Watch Co. Monday cut its group earnings estimates for this fiscal year through March 31 due to weaker-than-expected sales of watches, and information and electronics equipment. ("Japan Citizen Watch Cuts FY Grp Net Pft Outlook To Y19.5B," ran at 0717 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Spot prices of dynamic random access memory chips continue to decline, as demand for personal computers in China slows. China's main PC-buying season, the Lunar New Year holiday, is over, and it's the last major boomlet for DRAM makers before the weakest season of the year, which extends from February to June. (Asia DRAM Report to run by around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's banking and securities regulators Sunday issued trial rules mainly aimed at restricting risks that commercial banks may face while they tap into the previously off-limit fund management sector. ("China Issues Trial Rules On Banks Launching Fund Mgmt Ops," ran at 0521 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =Asian Stock Focus: Signs of a turnaround in long-dormant consumer demand are reviving interest in South Korea's major retailers. Share prices of the two major listed retailers - Shinsegae and Hyundai Department Store - have raced ahead on the back of successful January sales promotions. According to government statistics, big Korean retailers reported year-to-year sales increases of between 6% and 9.7% in the winter-sales period. ("=Asian Stock Focus: Investors Notice S Korean Retail Stks," ran at 0236 GMT) [ 21-02-05 0923GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Criticizing the world's major iron ore miners, Australia's Bluescope Steel Ltd. Monday said first half net profit more than doubled and could secure a similar result in the final six months with a surcharge due to soaring costs. ("=UPDATE: Australia's Bluescope Steel May Impose Surcharge," ran at 0846 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand's Sky Network Television Ltd.'s first-half net profit was almost four times higher than a year ago as it reaped the rewards of years of heavy capital expenditure, enabling it to grow its subscriber base at little cost. ("=UPDATE: NZ Sky TV Posts Record 1H Profit, Strong Growth," ran at 0131 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Sky City Entertainment Group said tight gaming regulations are hurting its earnings after the company Monday reported a fiscal first half net profit of NZ$57.1 million, up only modestly from NZ$55.1 million the previous year. ("=UPDATE: Gaming Rules Weigh On NZ Sky City's 1H Profit," ran at 2315 GMT; see also "=INTERVIEW: NZ SKY City Eyes Auckland, Australia Growth" at 0428 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thailand's Khon Kaen Sugar Industry PCL plans to sell 280 million new shares at THB4.30 a share at an initial public offering Thursday and Friday, the financial adviser for the IPO said Monday. ("Thai Khon Kaen Sugar To Sell IPO Shrs At THB4.30/Shr Thu," ran at 0853 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 PHNOM PENH: For Australia & New Zealand Banking Group's newest joint venture partner, Cambodia's plunging economic growth rates, chronic political instability and poor infrastructure offer a fine opportunity for an international-standard bank keen to tap the hidden savings of the county's nascent middle class. ("=INTERVIEW:ANZ Bk Cambodia JV Seeks Profits In The Ruins," ran at 0525 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore's G.K. Goh Holdings Ltd. said it plans a special payout of S$59 million to shareholders from the sale of its stockbroking and research businesses to Malaysian investment bank CIMB Bhd. for S$239 million. ("Singapore GK Goh Plans Special Div From Brokerage Sale," ran at 0302 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's GM Daewoo Auto & Technology Co. Monday unveiled a new version of the Matiz, the country's most popular minicar, and announced it aims to sell 50,000 units of the new vehicle in the local market this year. ("S Korea GM Daewoo Launches New Version Of Matiz Minicar," ran at 0445 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 =JAPAN POWER: Japan's top refiner, Nippon Oil Corp., aims to diversify its business portfolio, including taking a bigger role in electricity generation and power retailing. Among the factors driving this change in strategy is a long-term decline in Japan's demand for heavy fuel oil, as manufacturing companies shift \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  offshore to cheaper locations like China. Other reasons are power-sector liberalization, a greater reliance on nuclear power and environmental pressures to switch to cleaner fuel, a Nippon Oil official said. ("=JAPAN POWER: Nippon Oil Sets Its Sights On Power Sector," ran at 0604 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's agricultural sector posted a trade deficit of $4.64 billion last year, swinging from a surplus of $2.50 billion in 2003, according to the Ministry of Agriculture. ("China '04 Ag Trade Deficit $4.64B Vs '03 Surplus $2.5B," first take ran at 0733 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Fitch Ratings said the New Zealand government must clarify the regulatory risks that energy utilities face if the country hopes to secure enough investment in generation to overcome potential energy shortages. ("=INTERVIEW: NZ's Energy Rules Must Be Clarified - Fitch" ran at 2343 GMT) [ 21-02-05 0923GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050221e12l000cn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
