\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Tsy's Taylor Calls Senate Bill On China 'Protectionist'  \b0\par\par\uc2 347 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2005\par 21:05\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2005, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Feb 11, 2005 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 A Senate bill calling for a tariff on all imports from China if negotiations to revalue China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  don't succeed is "protectionist," a top U.S. Treasury Department official said Friday in an interview aired live on Bloomberg Television. Sens. Charles Schumer, D-N.Y., and Lindsey Graham, R-S.C., recently introduced a measure that calls for a 180-day period to negotiate revaluation of the yuan and, if the talks fail, a new 27.5% tariff on all imports from China. "We hope that that bill is not something that has much chance of passing because it would be protectionist," U.S. Treasury Undersecretary for International Affairs John Taylor said in the interview. "We think the approach that we are on now is much more satisfactory and much more beneficial to the United States and our economy and the creation of jobs," Taylor said. The Bush administration has been pressing China to shift to a flexible exchange rate. China has indicated that it is committed to shifting to a floating exchange rate over the long term. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightly manages its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , both through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and capital controls, effectively pegging it at 8.3 yuan to the dollar. U.S. manufacturers want China to revalue to a stronger rate, arguing the yuan is undervalued, giving Chinese producers an unfair competitive advantage. "There is a lot of progress now in our discussions with China to move to a flexible exchange rate and we had good meetings in London with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor and the finance minister on the steps they are taking," Taylor said in the Bloomberg interview. Asked about the ever-widening U.S. trade deficit with China, Taylor said getting China to move to a more flexible exchange rate would help the imbalance. "Protectionism is not a solution to problems like this," Taylor said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Deborah Lagomarsino, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9255; deborah.lagomarsino@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020050211e12b0069q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
