\par\par \b \uc2 BC-MARKETS-ASIA- \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES \highlight\uc2 -FACTBOX.  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,167 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2005\par 07:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Feb 1 (Reuters) - Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes are in the spotlight ahead of this weekend's Group of Seven meeting in London, which is expected to encourage China and its neighbours to let their exchange rates rise to promote a reduction in global economic imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 In general, emerging Asian economies explicitly or implicitly shadow the dollar. But the regimes vary, from the Malaysian ringgit's rigid peg to South Korea's independently floating won.  \par \par \uc2 The following summary, drawn from official sources and investment bank reports, is based on the International Monetary Fund's definitions for fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegs, managed floats with varying degrees of fluctuations, and independent floats.  \par \par \uc2 FIXED PEGS  \par \par \uc2 -  \par \par \uc2 MALAYSIAN RINGGIT  \par \par \uc2 The ringgit has been fixed at 3.8 to the dollar since September 1998. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  used to float until a flight of capital and a collapse in its value during the 1997/98 Asian financial crisis prompted the authorities to impose capital controls and fix the ringgit.  \par \par \uc2 Markets have been abuzz with talk, denied by ministers, that the time is ripe to review the continued usefulness of the peg.  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG DOLLAR  \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar is pegged at 7.8 per U.S. dollar as part of a \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Board Arrangement that was created on October 17, 1983, in response to a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis.  \par \par \uc2 Under the board's rules, the Hong Kong dollar monetary base has to be matched by equivalent foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.  \par \par \uc2 The system is asymmetric, requiring the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  to guarantee the sale of U.S. dollars at a fixed rate of 7.8 Hong Kong dollars. But the HKMA is not obliged to buy dollars at that rate: in October 2003, when yuan revaluation speculation was rife, the HK dollar rose to 7.7 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The peg requires the HKMA to adopt U.S. monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is fully convertible on the capital account.  \par \par \uc2 CHINESE YUAN OR RENMINBI  \par \par \uc2 China's yuan is a closely managed float that has pegged the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a 0.04 percent band centred on a rate of around 8.278 per dollar since the Asian crisis.  \par \par \uc2 China had followed a dual exchange rate since 1979 but unified those rates in 1994 to create a crawling yuan peg.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan appreciated from 8.7 per dollar to around 8.3 between 1994 and 1996, and continued to fluctuate around that level until the Asian crisis, when Beijing rejected advice to devalue like its neighbours and virtually fixed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  instead.  \par \par \uc2 Beijing says the stability this created provided a platform for Asia's rapid economic recovery and is an argument for not being rushed now in the other direction into a revaluation.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily, buying and selling yuan to preserve the peg. The yuan is freely traded on the trade account but the capital account is relatively closed, except for foreign direct investment and a few lending and borrowing transactions.  \par \par \uc2 Still, companies and individuals have found ways round the capital controls and have poured "hot money" into China on speculation of a policy change that will loosen the peg and result in a stronger yuan.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan has an active non-deliverable forwards (NDF) market, an unofficial derivatives market where dollar-denominated forward contracts are used to bet on the yuan's future value.  \par \par \uc2 MANAGED FLOATS:  \par \par \uc2 -  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE DOLLAR  \par \par \uc2 Tweaking the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's managed float has been the primary policy tool of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS) since 1981. Economists say the float is a combination of the band, basket and crawl systems.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS conducts monetary policy by letting the Sing dollar's nominal effective exchange rate (NEER) appreciate or depreciate, either by shifting the mid-point of the undisclosed band or adjusting its width. The composition of the basket is secret, but economists say the dollar and dollar-bloc \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  make up the biggest proportion.  \par \par \uc2 Policy shifted from neutral to a tightening bias in April 2004 via a gradual appreciation of the mid-point of the NEER.  \par \par \uc2 The Sing dollar is freely convertible on the trade account and has very minimal restrictions on capital flows.  \par \par \uc2 INDIAN RUPEE  \par \par \uc2 Economists say the Reserve Bank of India uses a monitoring band for the rupee, which allows it to float within a band determined by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of India's top five trading partners, adjusted for inflation levels.  \par \par \uc2 Markets believe the width of the band is five percent on either side of this neutral real effective exchange rate (REER), and the RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the band is breached.  \par \par \uc2 The RBI regularly publishes data on the REER base period and neutral value, but with a lag.  \par \par \uc2 The current unified exchange rate was adopted in 1993 after a balance of payments crisis led to devaluations in 1991.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee is not fully convertible on the capital account. There are still some limits on residents borrowing overseas, on residents converting local assets into foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on the access foreigners have to Indian debt markets.  \par \par \uc2 THAI BAHT  \par \par \uc2 Massive capital outflows forced Thailand to abandon the baht's peg against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on July 2, 1997, triggering a big devaluation against the dollar that marked the onset of the Asian financial crisis.  \par \par \uc2 Thailand adopted a managed float. Like other Asian central banks operating under similar regimes, the Bank of Thailand is averse to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  often in the market.  \par \par \uc2 Selective capital controls exist. There are strict limits on baht positions held by foreigners and offshore trading in baht is negligible.  \par \par \uc2 INDONESIAN RUPIAH  \par \par \uc2 Indonesia switched to a freely floating rupiah from a managed float in August 1997 during the Asian crisis. Several exchange controls remain.  \par \par \uc2 INDEPENDENTLY FLOATING:  \par \par \uc2 -   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par KOREAN WON\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The won was floated in December 1997 and is one of Asia's most actively traded \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Korea runs an inflation-targeting policy, but the government dictates \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy and keeps a watchful eye on the won's competitiveness. This occasionally leads to strains between the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the finance ministry.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreigners have greater access to South Korean capital markets than to most others in emerging Asia, yet there are some restrictions on offshore won trading and on local banks' positions in won. The won NDF market that has developed as a consequence is among the most liquid in Asia.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen-won is a popular cross rate, traded in the belief that Seoul likes to keep the won competitive versus the yen because of the overlap between South Korean and Japanese exports.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 PHILIPPINE PESO  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The peso is allowed to trade in a wider band than most other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and is prone to volatile swings in response to political developments. It is also sensitive to data on the Philippines' large fiscal deficit and on remittances from overseas Filipinos, a major source of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , with limited official reserves, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  occasionally. It also adjusts reserve requirements, and the rate at which they are remunerated, to help smooth the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050201e1210066a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
