\par\par \b \uc2 Daily Chinese yuan update.  \b0\par\par\uc2 326 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2005\par 09:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Jan 12 (Reuters) - Following is a summary of market activity and news articles relating to the Chinese yuan on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  *** The yuan  ended unchanged at 8.2765 per U.S.\par  dollar, near the stronger end of its managed trading range.\par  *** The one-year non-deliverable dollar forward discount\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 versus the yuan was at 4,350 points, implying a rate of 7.843 yuan per dollar in 12 months' time.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NDFs are a transaction where a forward price is agreed between a customer and a bank, but settlement on the value date is undertaken entirely in U.S. dollars.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 *** One-year implied yuan volatility was at 7.75/8.10 percent.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Implied volatility is a measure of how much the options market expects the price of the underlying asset to move during the life of the option.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yuan moves in a band of 8.2760 to 8.2800 enforced by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Turnover, a strong $1.85 billion on Tuesday, was not immediately available. The yuan weakened to 8.0134 against 100 Japanese yen from 7.9593 on Tuesday and softened against the euro to 10.8612 from 10.8489.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OPEN MARKET ACTIVITY  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  issued a record 90 billion yuan ($11 billion) in bills and siphoned off another 5 billion yuan via repos on Tuesday, as Beijing stepped up efforts to siphon off funds resulting from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEWS  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ** U.S. Evans urges flexibility for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's yuan  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BEIJING - Outgoing U.S. Commerce Secretary Don Evans on Wednesday urged China to adopt a market-driven exchange rate, saying it was the best policy to ensure long-term growth and prosperity.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Full Story:  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - ~- --  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ** China inflation eased further in December -sources  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BEIJING - Inflation in China was slightly below 4 percent in 2004 as price rises slowed further in December, reducing the chance of raising interest rates any time soon, government sources and analysts said on Tuesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Full Story:  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - ~- --.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050112e11c0091b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
