\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Central Banks Stand Tall As Dollar Buyers  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Robert Flint  \par \uc2 964 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2004\par 03:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--It's hard to know exactly who sold the dollar during its recent decline, but at least we know who's still buying the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - the world's central banks.  \par \par \uc2 Despite doomsday prophecies of a flight from dollar-denominated assets that could lead to a catastrophic dollar meltdown, there's little evidence that any such move is underway. Central banks still continue to put the bulk of their reserves in dollars, keeping their appetites for U.S. paper at a substantial level.  \par \par \uc2 What's more, it's highly unlikely that central banks will ever participate in a wholesale liquidation of dollar reserves. They 'would be shooting themselves in the foot' if they did, said Carl Weinberg, chief economist at High Frequency Economics in Valhalla, New York.  \par \par \uc2 The fears about a dump of dollar reserves stem from the imbalances in the U.S. economy, namely the enormous deficits in the current account federal budget. Supposedly, mounting difficulties for the U.S. to finance its deficits will send the dollar spiraling downward until it crashes. The selloff is premised on a massive dollar selloff, including from central banks.  \par \par \uc2 However, Weinberg and others have pointed out that central banks, unlike hedge funds, don't have a profit incentive. Their mandate, usually based on national law, is to keep prices, exchange rates and financial markets stable, while preserving - not maximizing - the value of foreign reserves.  \par \par \uc2 'Central banks have massive holdings of U.S. fixed income securities which they cannot sell without risking a large negative price impact, thus affecting the valuation of the remaining dollar reserves,' wrote analysts at Goldman Sachs Economic Research in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 Moreover, recent developments on the interest-rate front around the world give central banks even less inclination to shift away from dollars. The central banks of the U.K., Sweden, South Korea, South Africa, Chile and New Zealand all announced decisions to keep rates unchanged Thursday, while Canada and Australia stood pat earlier in the week and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held steady last week.  \par \par \uc2 If the Federal Reserve hikes rates after its next policy-setting meeting on Dec. 14 - like everyone expects - then the U.S. will enjoy a positive interest-rate differential to the euro zone, which would theoretically increase the attractiveness of U.S. debt.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      Reserve Status Sound \par  \par   Figures from the International Monetary Fund, published in an appendix to its Annual Report for 2004, indicated 63.8% of the world's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves were held in dollars as of April 30 this year, compared with 63.5% the previous fiscal year. Official holdings of dollars peaked in 2001 at 66.9% after rising steadily from 53.1% in 1994. \par   While there are signs that some adjustment of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  portfolios may be underway to better reflect current economic realities and trade relationships, it hardly amounts to a shunning of the dollar. 'The reallocation of U.S. dollar denominated reserves is a rather slow process,' the Goldman Sachs analysts noted. \par   'Smaller Asian and oil exporting countries have been at the forefront' of the trend to diversify \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  holdings, while 'Japan likely remains the most conservative reserve manager with a very large majority of reserves still held in U.S. dollars,' they added. \par   Neither Japan nor China, the biggest holders of U.S. debt, have shown any tendency to sell. Weinberg at High Frequency Economics noted that ' the U.S. yield curve shows it is flattening as the Fed(eral Reserve) hikes rates and the recent trend in ten-year yields and longer has been steady or even downward.' \par   If central banks had been selling Treasurys on a big scale, a 'bump up in yield and down in price' should have manifested itself. With ten-year Treasury yields just north of 4.15%, Weinberg said he's 'hard pressed to believe' there's a selloff underway. \par   On Thursday, UBS listed a number of examples of recent acquisitions of dollars by central banks. The Bank of Korea picked up a record number of (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) reserves last month while the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's reserves also 'increased significantly' by over $5 billion in November, it said. \par   'This trend seems to be continuing with this week's five-year U.S. Treasury auction seeing indirect bids take up 65.8% of the total bids,' it said. 'These are likely to be from central banks recycling their (dollar) \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  proceeds.' \par   In addition, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reported that its reserves had jumped by at least $25.5 billion to exceed $540 billion as of the end of October and Russia said its reserves had hit a new high of $121.6 billion. \par  \par     Cold Comfort \par  \par   It boils down to no one taking much comfort from a sudden and drastic drop in the dollar. Exporting countries want to keep their goods competitive in world markets, so they buy dollars to keep their own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from appreciating. They then lend that money back to the U.S., allowing U.S. consumers to continue buying imported goods. \par   That's how the U.S. has managed to finance its current account deficit. No less than Fed chairman Alan Greenspan recently warned than in the long run, the world's appetite for U.S. assets is finite. \par   No one knows at exactly what point the U.S. deficits will become unsustainable, but there's still no evidence it's happening now. \par  \par   (Robert Flint, an assistant news editor, has covered \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets for Dow Jones Newswires for the past seven years and was previously Beijing bureau chief and chief correspondent in Scandinavia.) \par  \par    -Robert Flint; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4408; robert.flint@dowjones.com \par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020041210e0ca002ut\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
