\par\par \b \uc2 Asian  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves rise in November.  \b0\par\par\uc2 461 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Dezember 2004\par 07:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     SINGAPORE, Dec 8 (Reuters) - Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserve\par holdings, excluding China, rose by $36.8 billion to $1.81\par trillion in November, the latest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  figures show.\par     China was not included in the total because it has not\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 released figures for October or November. Including China's  \f28 \par \par \uc2 September holdings of $514.5 billion, Asian reserves would  \par \par \uc2 total $2.32 trillion.  \par \par \uc2 South Korea, India and Singapore showed the largest  \par \par \uc2 increases in reserves during the month, most of which markets  \par \par \uc2 suspect was due to dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rein in  \par \par \uc2 rapidly rising local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Analysts suspect that nearly $10 billion of the $14.2  \par \par \uc2 billion rise in Korea's November reserves was on account of  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the rest explained by changes in the values of  \par \par \uc2 the euro, yen and other assets and coupon flows.  \par \par \uc2 Markets are also abuzz with speculation Asian central banks  \par \par \uc2 are considering diversifying their reserves to avoid the  \par \par \uc2 erosion of the dollar, which analysts estimate make up some 70  \par \par \uc2 percent of reserve assets in Asia.  \par \par \uc2 China's reserves rose by $111 billion in the first nine  \par \par \uc2 months of the year. Outside of China, the pace of reserves  \par \par \uc2 growth had slowed markedly since the end of the first quarter,  \par \par \uc2 until November.  \par \par \uc2 Asian reserves have risen by $164.7 billion in the eight  \par \par \uc2 months to the end of November, after rising by $247.2 billion  \par \par \uc2 in the first quarter of 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say the change since March shows that Asian  \par \par \uc2 central banks outside of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  have not had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the  \par \par \uc2 same extent to stem \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains as they did in 2002 and  \par \par \uc2 2003.  \par \par \uc2 Latest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve holdings:   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                Reserves       As at      end-2003   percent\par                   $ bln                     $ bln    change\par  Japan            840.1      Nov 30         673.5     +24.7\par  China*           514.5      Sep 30         403.3     +27.6\par  Taiwan           239.1      Nov 30         206.6     +15.7\par  South Korea      192.6      Nov 30         155.4     +23.9\par  India            126.9      Nov 26         100.6     +26.2\par  Hong Kong        121.5      Nov 30         118.4      +2.6\par  Singapore        110.1      Nov 30          96.3     +14.3\par  Malaysia          60.9      Nov 12          44.9     +35.6\par  Thailand          48.1      Nov 26          42.1     +14.3\par  Indonesia         35.7      Nov 23          36.3      -1.7\par  Philippines       15.8      Nov 30          16.8      -6.0\par  Pakistan          12.1      Nov 20          12.0      +0.7\par  Bangladesh         3.2      Nov 30           2.6     +21.5\par  Total          2,320.7                   1,908.8     +21.6\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 *In December 2003, China used $45 billion of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 reserves - about 10 percent of its holdings at that time - to  \f28 