\par\par \b \uc2 DJ China Big 4 Bks May Be Market Makers In USD Trading-Paper  \b0\par\par\uc2 361 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2004\par 21:04\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2004, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Nov 28, 2004 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymakers may allow the country's four biggest state banks to become market makers in U.S. dollar trading, a move that could reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Business Post reports. The report, which didn't cite named sources, says the draft measures for such a move have been completed and it is now down to the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , China's top \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator, to announce them. China's four biggest banks: Industrial & Commercial Bank of China (ICBC.YY), Agricultural Bank of China (AGBC.YY), China Construction Bank (CCB.YY) and Bank of China (BCH.YY), may be among the first market makers to be approved, the paper says. And Bank of China is the most likely to get first approval, it says. To keep trading fluid, a market maker agrees to be a counterparty, either as a buyer or seller, in a financial transaction when no other party can be found. Currently, the People's Bank of China is the key market maker in dollar trading in the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market due to a policy that keeps the yuan convertible only for trade-related purposes, and that keeps the local unit mostly pegged to the dollar at around 8.277. The report also says \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  accounts for a large portion of M2, China's broadest measure of money supply. If the proportion of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in money supply diminishes, then the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's regular open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  will become the key factor in money supply adjustments, the report says. Xia Bin, an official in a research department under the State Council - China's cabinet - said China's foreign reserves have exceeded reasonable levels. By introducing market makers, China's growth in foreign reserves can decelerate, which would be "very welcome," Xia is reported as saying in the report.  \par \par \uc2 Newspaper Web site:  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.caijingshibao.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.caijingshibao.com}}}\par \par \uc2 -By China Bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; (86-21) 6218 3268; djnews.shanghai@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020041129e0bs0018k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
