\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Oct 12  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,557 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2004\par 11:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Temasek Holdings plans to reduce its exposure to Singapore to just one-third over 10 years and diversify in Asia and developed economies, the state-owned investment company said in its first-ever public annual report. The report confirmed Temasek is by far the biggest shareholder in Singapore with total assets of S$180 billion and some S$9.32 billion in cash. The potrfolio value of its assets - listed and private - amounts to S$90 billion. ("Temasek Says To Cut Singapore Exposure, Grow In Asia," ran at 0900 GMT; UPDATE to run around 1130 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Government land auction ongoing. (First update expected around 1000 GMT, followed by second update after press conferences by the government and winning bidders).  \par \par \uc2 ASIA DEBT: China has finalized the indicative offering size and the marketing plan for its keenly-awaited global sovereign bond, setting the stage for a potentially bitter battle for investors with a skillful South Korean rival and a regional development bank. (ASIA DEBT to run by 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CHINA WATCH: The Taiwan government has invited China to discuss historic two-way, non-stop charter flights between the political rivals, but pundits say Beijing has little incentive to respond before the island's legislative elections in December. Any progress in cross-Strait relations would only increase the chances of a victory for Taiwan President Chen Shui-bian's ruling party, which Beijing views as pro- independence. (CHINA WATCH to run by 1130 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: The rise of Christian right party Family First could influence the Australian political landscape and policy making under the government of Prime Minister John Howard. (Story to run by around 1100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China Tuesday adopted the 7-day bond repurchase rate as its reference benchmark interest rate for money market trading in another step toward introducing a market-based interest rate system. ("China Adopts 7-Day Repurchase Rate As Mkt Reference Rate," ran at 0315 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Contrary to what economic textbooks would say, as Japan's economy continues to improve the yen might weaken rather than strengthen, some analysts say. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Japan Recovery May Hurt, Not Help, Yen," ran at 0908 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: The Philippines decided Tuesday to abandon its plan to negotiate a deal over the concession to operate the power transmission assets of state utility National Power Corp. and will instead conduct a public bidding to simplify the privatization process, which is still expected to be completed by December. ("Philippines Opts To Hold Auction For Transco Concession," ran at 0636 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's consumer sentiment improved for the first time in five months in September as a bullish equity market raised hopes for income gains amid a prolonged slowdown in domestic demand, government data showed Tuesday. ("S Korea's Sep Consumer Sentiment Index 88.9 Vs 87 Aug," ran at 2253 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Economy and Fiscal Policy Minister Heizo Takenaka said Tuesday the economy remains on a recovery track despite some signs of weakness in recent data. ("Japan Takenaka: Econ Correcting Slightly Amid Uptrend," first take ran at 0454 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: Industrial growth in India rose 7.9% on year in August, signaling gathering momentum in consumer and investment demand and renewing concerns about inflation. (UPDATE due by 1000 GMT).  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's Ministry of Commerce Tuesday predicted the country's consumer price index will likely rise by about 4% year on year in 2004, up from 1.2% in the previous year due to higher grain prices. ("China's Commerce Ministry Sees 2004 CPI Up 4% On Year," ran at 0626 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australian business conditions remained robust in September, pushing wage costs and overall prices higher, according to National Australia Bank's latest business survey released Tuesday. ("Australia NAB Business Conditions -1 Pt To 15 Pts In Sep," ran at 0130 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese life insurer Daido Life Insurance Co. will remain bullish on stocks in the second fiscal half through March as it expects the Japanese and global economy to stay strong. ("=INTERVIEW: Daido Life Remains Bullish On Equities In 2H," ran at 0309 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Confidence among New Zealand's businesses remained negative but improved in the third quarter, while inflationary pressures remained at an all-time high, according to a survey released Tuesday. ("NZ 3Q QSBO Business Confidence Improves From 2Q" ran at 2127 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Confidence about jobs and wages among New Zealand employees increased in the third quarter from the second, pointing to solid wages growth and consumer spending in the current quarter, according to a survey released Tuesday. ("NZ Employee Confidence Up In 3Q; Wage Rises Anticipated" ran at 1200 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Bank of Korea Governor Park Seung indicated Tuesday that the South Korean government, along with financial authorities in other Asian countries, should scale back \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, particularly given their ample current account surpluses. ("Bank Of Korea Gov Calls For Less Govt \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," ran at 2100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  will speed up its reform of state-owned enterprises and make the process more transparent, said Vice Premier Huang Ju, according to a state media report. ("China Vice Premier: State Co Reform To Speed Up - Xinhua," ran at 0050 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Bank of Thailand has received a report about 11 newly classified nonperforming loan cases at Krung Thai Bank PCL and will study it to determine whether the board of the state-owned bank was involved in any wrongdoing, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said Tuesday. ("Thai Ctrl Bk Will Study Krung Thai Bd's Role In NPL Cases," ran at 0810 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Daiei Inc. faces a deadline to decide on whether to tap the government-backed Industrial Revitalization Corp. of Japan for help in its restructuring. Media reports indicate the debt-heavy supermarket operator plans to opt for a private-sector-led rehabilitation, against the will of its bankers. (Story likely later)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: Infosys Technologies Ltd., India's bellwether technology stock, Tuesday posted a 49% rise in net profit for the second quarter and again raised its earnings forecast for the full year, underscoring its resistance to the outsourcing backlash in the U.S., its main market. (UPDATE due 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: Tata Consultancy Services to announce second-quarter results, its first such announcement since listing late August. (Story to run late Tuesday)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Posco, the world's fifth-largest steel maker in terms of output, Tuesday posted a record third-quarter net profit ended Sept. 30 of KRW1.01 trillion, which was boosted by solid steel prices at home and abroad. ("S Korea Posco 3Q Net KRW1.01T Vs KRW499.6B," ran at 0706 GMT; UPDATE to run by 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Inpex Corp., a Japanese government-owned oil developer, has received approval to list on the Tokyo Stock Exchange on Nov. 17, the TSE said Tuesday. The state-owned Japan National Oil Corp. holds a 53.96% stake in the oil developer. The TSE also said it has approved Japanese memory microchip producer Elpida Memory Inc. to list on the bourse on Nov. 15, while NTT Urban Development Co. said Tuesday it plans to raise up to Y59.4 billion in an initial public offering of shares that will mark the third flotation of a Nippon Telegraph & Telephone Corp. unit. (UPDATE by 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: State-owned Bank of China and China Construction Bank are expected to go public next year, said China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Zhou Xiaochuan. ("Bank Of China, Construction Bank To List In 2005 - PBOC," ran at 0301 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: Investors snapped up shares of Thai fixed line operator True Corp. PCL in a relief rally after the company completed the long-delayed debt restructuring of its loss-making mobile phone operator TA Orange Ltd., but the longer term view of the stock remains negative given True's massive debt and a pending capital increase. ("=ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: Thai True Rally Likely Short-lived," ran at 0000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: The value of mergers and acquisitions in Asia Pacific, excluding Japan, jumped more than 30% on year to $121 billion during the first nine months of this year, led by transactions involving Australian, Singaporean and Chinese companies, a Morgan Stanley official said Tuesday. ("M&As In Asia Pacific Jumps 30% Jan-Sep '04-Morgan Stanley," ran at 0505 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China has streamlined the approval process for local companies planning to invest overseas in a move that further opens the door for cash-rich enterprises to expand their international presence. ("China Eases Way For Overseas Investment By Local Firms," ran at 0819 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Sumitomo Metal Mining Co. and Sumitomo Corp. said in a press release Tuesday they will invest a combined $265 million to $334 million in the Cerro Verde copper mine in Peru. ("Sumitomo Cos To Invest Up To $334M In Peru Copper Mine," ran at 0300 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Creditors of Thai Petrochemical Industry PCL Tuesday approved the company's revised debt restructuring plan, which calls for the sale of a stake in the indebted company to a strategic investor.("Thai TPI Creditors Approve Revised Debt Plan," first take ran at 0413 GMT; UPDATE to run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: The Australian Competition Tribunal Tuesday approved plans for a proposed alliance between Air New Zealand Ltd. and Qantas Airways Ltd., but it's unclear what form that alliance will take since the proposal has already been grounded by a New Zealand High Court ruling last month. ("=UPDATE:Qantas, Air NZ Eye New Alliance After Tribunal OK," ran at 0509 GMT) [ 12-10-04 0924GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: With fuel prices hovering at record highs, Qantas Airways Ltd. said Tuesday it is looking likely it will have to increase its existing passenger fuel surcharge. ("=INTERVIEW: Qantas Near Certain To Raise Fuel Surcharge," first take ran at 0029 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australian property development and investment company Mirvac Group launched Tuesday a friendly scrip-based takeover for James Fielding Group. ("Australia's Mirvac To Acquire James Fielding Group," ran at 0740 GMT; UPDATE to run by around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Hitachi Ltd. said Tuesday its California-based hard-disc drive unit plans to fire 10% of its U.S. employees by the end of this year. ("Hitachi's HDD Unit To Fire 10% Of US Workers By Year-End," ran at 0656 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Toshiba Corp. said Tuesday it plans to begin marketing DVD players in the U.S. using a next-generation DVD format in October-December of 2005 at a retail price of $999. ("Toshiba To Launch HD DVD Player In Oct-Dec 2005 In US," ran at 0619 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Nasdaq-listed Chindex International Inc. will seek to cash in on its SARS-epidemic era reputation as China's premium private health care provider with the launch later this month of it second hospital in the eastern city of Shanghai, a company executive said Tuesday. (INTERVIEW to run by 1100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Australia's two biggest states on Tuesday unveiled tougher anti-smoking laws for pubs, gaming venues and other indoor public spaces over the next three years, a move that may hamper sales growth at Australia's largest gaming and pub companies ("=UPDATE: Australia's Biggest States Extend Smoking Bans," ran at 0455 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: FamilyMart Co. plans to open a store in Los Angeles around next May, the first step in a planned expansion into the U.S. market, a spokesman for the Japanese convenience store operator said Tuesday. ("Japan's FamilyMart Plans To Open Store In L.A. Next May," ran at 0119 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese department store operator Takashimaya Co. said Tuesday its group net profit plunged 67.9% on year in the first half after pension-related profits boosted its earnings last year. ("Japan's Takashimaya 1H Group Net Profit Y5.4B Vs Y16.8B," first take ran at 0556 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Vector, New Zealand's biggest electricity lines network company, won't breakup the assets of distribution and metering concern NGC Holdings after it takes control because it says each of NGC's businesses will compliment its own \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("=INTERVIEW: NZ's Vector Won't Seek To Breakup NGC Hldgs" ran at 0044 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Gullivers Travel Group, New Zealand's largest outbound travel agent, said Tuesday it is considering an initial public offering and listing on the New Zealand share market. ("NZ's Gullivers Travel Group Says Considering IPO For Nov," ran at 0235 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: SembCorp Logistics Ltd.'s shares took a battering Tuesday as the market fretted about the blow to its earnings from the sale of its 20% stake in Swiss sea freight company Kuehne & Nagel AG. ("=UPDATE: Singapore SembCorp Log Shrs Dn On Earnings Fears," ran at 0606 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 =CHINA ENERGY WATCH: In a US$40 billion dash for gas, China is firing up its investment in Australia's booming energy sector. Up to recently, most of China's dabbling Down Under has been of the minerals variety. Now, amid record energy prices, China is swooping on Australia's vast offshore natural gas resources to feed its newfound enthusiasm for liquefied natural gas as an alternative power source. By the end of the decade around 40% of China's LNG imports will come from Australia. ("=CHINA ENERGY WATCH: China Fires Up Australia Gas Deals," ran at 0157 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thailand's cabinet Tuesday approved a 900-million-baht package of measures aiming at eradicating the presence of the H5N1 avian influenza virus in the country. (Story to run shortly)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: China's fledging fuel oil futures market appears to have taken off, thanks to a recent surge in activity following weeks of lethargic trade, but it's still too early to celebrate a successful launch as many key players are keeping clear of the market.("=Bright Future, Or False Dawn, For China HSFO Futures?," ran at 0354 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: The cost of grain in China is expected to have less influence on the country's consumer price index as an improved harvest helps to stabilize prices, analysis by the People's Bank of China research bureau has found. ("China PBOC Research Finds Less Grain Price Impact On CPI," ran at 0252 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =KOREA REFINERY WATCH: Low domestic stocks of heating oil are driving South Korean refiners to continue maximizing runs and to cut back exports of kerosene and gasoil in November. ("=KOREA REFINERY WATCH: High Runs, Low Heating Oil Stocks," ran at 0425 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Wuhan Steel Processing Co., a major steel maker in China, said Tuesday it expects its net profit in the January-September period at least quadrupled from the same period in 2003. ("China Wuhan Steel Expects Big Increase In Jan-Sep Profit," ran at 0103 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 PERTH: A proposed visit to Seoul this weekend by Western Australian premier Geoff Gallop has fueled speculation that Australia is still in the running to capture part of a US$30 billion South Korean liquefied natural gas tender. (Story to run shortly) [ 12-10-04 0924GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041012e0ac000e2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
