\par\par \b \uc2 Asian  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves edge up in September.  \b0\par\par\uc2 454 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2004\par 04:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     SINGAPORE, Oct 8 (Reuters) - Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserve\par holdings excluding China rose by $12.5 billion to $1.74\par trillion in September, latest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  figures show.\par     China was not included in the total because it has not\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2  released figures for August or September. Including China's   \par \par \uc2  July holdings of $483 billion, Asian reserves total $2.23   \par \par \uc2  trillion.   \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to release September   \par \par \uc2  figures later this month. In the first seven months of the year   \par \par \uc2  China's reserves rose by $80 billion, and it is the only   \par \par \uc2  country to have maintained a fairly consistent rate of increase   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Outside of China, the pace of reserves growth has slowed\par markedly since the end of the first quarter.\par     Asian reserves excluding China have risen by $28.1 billion\par in the six months to the end of August, after rising by $210.7\par billion in the first quarter of 2004.\par     Analysts say the change since March shows that Asian\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par central banks outside of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have not had to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to the\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par same extent to stem \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gains as they did in 2002 and\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     India became the fourth-largest holder of reserves in\par September, overtaking Hong Kong.\par     While Hong Kong's reserves of $118.4 billion are level from\par the end of 2003, India's reserves have risen by 18.1 percent to\par $118.8 billion -- although they have fallen slightly since May.\par     Latest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserve holdings:\par                Reserves       As at      end-2003   percent\par                   $ bln                     $ bln    change\par  Japan            831.0      Sep 30         673.5     +23.4\par  China*           483.0      Jul 31         403.3     +19.8\par  Taiwan           233.0      Sep 30         206.6     +12.8\par  South Korea      174.5      Sep 30         155.4     +12.3\par  Hong Kong        118.4      Sep 30         118.4       0.0\par  India            118.8      Sep 24         100.6     +18.1\par  Singapore        102.7      Sep 30          96.3      +6.6\par  Malaysia          55.4      Sep 15          44.9     +23.4\par  Thailand          44.3      Sep 24          42.1      +5.2\par  Indonesia         34.8      Sep 30          36.3      -4.1\par  Philippines       15.9      Sep 30          16.8      -5.4\par  Pakistan          12.4      Sep 25          12.0      +3.3\par  Bangladesh         3.1      Sep 30           2.6     +19.2\par  Total          2,227.3                   1,908.8     +16.7\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *In December 2003, China used $45 billion of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par reserves - about 10 percent of its holdings at that time - to\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par bail out two banks. Those funds are not included in this\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par figure.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sources: central banks.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Reporting by John Mair, editing by Christopher Kaufman;\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par john.mair@reuters.com; Reuters Messaging:\pa