\par\par \b \uc2 Tsy Quarles: China Should Move Faster To Flexible  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 585 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2004\par 15:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--U.S. Treasury officials are pressing China to move more quickly to a flexible exchange rate system, a shift that would allow more flexible exchange rates across Asia, U.S. Treasury Assistant Secretary of International Affairs Randal Quarles said Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "With its rapid economic growth and massive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, China is in a position to move quickly to a market-based exchange rate regime," Quarles told members of the National Association of Manufacturers, a group that has pressed the Bush Administration to take a hard line with China on the issue. U.S. manufacturers have said China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to the U.S. dollar undervalues the Chinese yuan and unfairly cheapens Chinese exports.  \par \par \uc2 Quarles said Treasury believes a change in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime would allow other Asian governments to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  less in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and also have more market based exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 "There is an acknowledgement that Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices are the fulcrum of the issue in that area of the world," Quarles said. "A change in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's practices brings the rest of the region along with it." [ 10-07-04 1008ET ]  \par \par \uc2 China's finance minister, Jin Renqing, and its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, Zhou Xiaochuan, were in Washington last weekend to participate in a regular joint U.S.-China meeting of economic policy-makers and to participate in a Group of Seven finance ministers' meeting. In a statement issued after the U.S.-China meeting, China affirmed its commitment to move "firmly and steadily" toward a flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system, but gave no timeframe for doing so.  \par \par \uc2 Quarles said Chinese officials are concerned that lifting capital controls and a fully liberalized \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system could trigger outflows from its weak banking system that could result in a weaker, not stronger Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Asked to estimate the proper market rate for the Chinese yuan, Quarles said it would probably be "somewhat higher." There isn't enough information to be more specific, he said.  \par \par \uc2 The Chinese have estimated their economy must create 10 million new jobs a year to keep up with population growth and to employ rural workers moving to the city, Quarles said. The government is wary of making changes that could destabilize the economy and jobs, and threaten political stability.  \par \par \uc2 Quarles played down the threat of an abrupt adjustment in the U.S. current account deficit. The current account deficit - the broadest measure of trade including capital flows - is now running above a 5% share of the economy. Many economists warn that is an unsustainable level and could lead to sharply higher interest rates and slower growth if foreign investors lose interest in U.S. assets.  \par \par \uc2 Although the U.S. has posted large current account deficits for several years, analysts are growing more worried now because a larger portion of the deficit is financed by purchases of U.S. government securities, rather than investment into the private sector.  \par \par \uc2 Quarles said the deficit would most likely decline slowly in an orderly fashion, and foreign governments aren't likely to suddenly reduce their purchases of U.S. Treasury bonds.  \par \par \uc2 "The general dynamics of the situation are such that it is unlikely governments would change their general appetite for U.S. securities," he said. U.S. markets are "liquid and deep" and could absorb changes in demand by a few players, he added.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9295; Elizabeth.Price@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 10-07-04 1047ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020041007e0a70001s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
